/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cloud-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cloud-plus", [["cp1",0,1,"A development team wants to deploy its web application code without patching operating systems or managing the language runtime, but it still wants to control the application itself. Which service model fits?",["Platform as a service","Infrastructure as a service with a hardened image","Software as a service","Colocation in a provider's data center"],0,"PaaS runs the OS and runtime for you while you deploy and manage your own code. IaaS leaves OS patching to the customer, and SaaS gives you someone else's finished application.","Domain 1: service models",[null,"With IaaS, even from a hardened image, the customer still owns guest OS patching and the runtime, which is exactly what the team wants to avoid.","SaaS delivers a finished application built by the vendor, so the team could not deploy or control its own code.","Colocation only rents rack space, power and cooling; the company would manage the hardware, OS and runtime itself."],2],["cp2",0,1,"A company runs Linux VMs on a public IaaS platform. An auditor asks who is responsible for applying security patches to the guest operating system. What is the correct answer?",["The cloud provider, as part of the hypervisor maintenance","The provider for critical patches and the customer for the rest","The customer","Whoever built the marketplace image the VM came from"],2,"Under the shared responsibility model for IaaS, the provider secures hardware and the hypervisor, and everything from the guest OS up is the customer's job. Choosing a marketplace image does not shift that duty.","Domain 1: shared responsibility",["The provider maintains the hypervisor and physical hosts, but in IaaS it has no responsibility for patching the guest OS inside customer VMs.","There is no such split in IaaS; the provider does not apply any patches, critical or otherwise, to a customer's guest OS.",null,"The image publisher only supplies the starting image; once the VM is launched, keeping its OS patched is the customer's duty."],1],["cp3",0,1,"A hospital keeps its patient database in its own data center for regulatory reasons but wants its public website to scale into a public cloud during flu season. Which deployment model describes this?",["Community cloud","Private cloud","Multicloud with two SaaS vendors","Hybrid cloud"],3,"Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private infrastructure with public cloud and connects them. Multicloud means using several public providers, which is not what is described.","Domain 1: deployment models",["A community cloud is infrastructure shared by several organizations with common requirements, not one hospital bridging its own data center and a public cloud.","A private cloud alone would not let the website burst into public cloud capacity during peaks.","Multicloud means using several public cloud or SaaS vendors; the scenario combines an on-premises data center with one public cloud.",null],3],["cp4",0,1,"Several state agencies with the same compliance requirements want to share one cloud environment that is not open to the general public. Which deployment model is this?",["Private cloud for each agency","Public cloud","Hybrid cloud","Community cloud"],3,"A community cloud is shared by organizations with common requirements such as regulation or mission. Giving each agency its own private cloud would not be shared.","Domain 1: deployment models",["Separate private clouds would give each agency its own isolated environment, so nothing would be shared among them.","A public cloud is open to any customer, but the agencies want an environment restricted to organizations like themselves.","Hybrid cloud describes connecting private or on-premises infrastructure with public cloud, not several organizations sharing one environment.",null],3],["cp5",0,1,"An application must keep running if one data center fails, and its database needs synchronous replication with very low latency. Where should the architect place the instances?",["In multiple availability zones within one region","In one availability zone on separate hosts","In two regions on different continents","On one dedicated host with redundant power supplies"],0,"Availability zones in a region are separate facilities joined by low-latency links, so synchronous replication works and a single data center failure is survived. Distant regions add too much latency for synchronous writes.","Domain 1: regions and zones",[null,"Separate hosts in a single availability zone still sit in one data center, so a facility failure would take everything down.","Regions on different continents add so much latency that synchronous database replication becomes impractical.","Redundant power supplies protect one server, but a single host in a single data center is still a single point of failure."],3],["cp6",0,1,"A licensing agreement requires that a vendor's software run on physical servers that are not shared with any other cloud customer. Which option meets this?",["Spot instances in a private subnet","Burstable instances with CPU credits","Dedicated hosts","A larger general-purpose instance size"],2,"Dedicated hosts give you a physical server used only by your account, which satisfies per-socket or per-core licensing and isolation rules. Instance size or network placement does not remove multitenancy.","Domain 1: compute and multitenancy",["Spot instances run on shared multitenant hardware and can be reclaimed; a private subnet only changes network placement, not physical isolation.","Burstable instances are cheap shared-tenancy VMs that earn CPU credits; they still share physical servers with other customers.",null,"A larger instance size is still a VM on shared hardware, so it does not guarantee a physical server used only by you."],2],["cp7",0,1,"For disaster recovery, a company keeps its core database continuously replicated to a second region, while application servers there remain as images that are launched only when a disaster is declared. Which DR approach is this?",["Warm standby","Backup and restore","Multisite active-active","Pilot light"],3,"Pilot light keeps only the critical core (usually data) running and live, and starts the rest on demand. Warm standby keeps a scaled-down but running copy of the full stack.","Domain 1: disaster recovery",["Warm standby keeps a scaled-down but running copy of the whole stack, including app servers, not just replicated data with images waiting to launch.","Backup and restore keeps only backups and rebuilds everything after a disaster; there is no continuously replicated live database.","Multisite active-active runs full production in both locations serving traffic at the same time, far more than idle images.",null],3],["cp8",0,1,"Twenty Linux web servers must all read and write the same set of uploaded documents through a standard mounted file system. Which storage type should be provisioned?",["Block storage volume attached to each server","Managed file storage accessed over NFS","Archive object storage","A local instance store on each server"],1,"Managed file storage provides a shared file system that many servers mount at once over NFS or SMB. A block volume normally attaches to one instance, and archive storage is for rarely read data.","Domain 1: storage types",["A standard block volume attaches to one instance at a time and has no shared file system semantics for twenty servers writing concurrently.",null,"Archive object storage is for rarely accessed data, has slow retrieval and is not mounted as a standard file system.","Instance store is local, temporary disk on each host, so every server would see its own separate copy and data is lost when the instance stops."],1],["cp9",0,1,"A media company needs to store billions of images, served to apps through HTTPS calls, with virtually unlimited capacity and pay-per-GB pricing. Which storage type is the best fit?",["Block storage","Object storage","File storage","Instance store volumes"],1,"Object storage scales almost without limit, is accessed through HTTP APIs and bills per GB stored. Block and file storage are provisioned for servers and do not suit billions of web-served objects.","Domain 1: storage types",["Block storage is provisioned as disk volumes attached to servers; it is not served over HTTPS to apps and does not scale to billions of objects cheaply.",null,"File storage exposes a mounted file system over NFS or SMB for servers, not an HTTP API with near-unlimited, pay-per-GB capacity.","Instance store volumes are temporary local disks tied to a single VM, so they lose data and cannot scale or serve content directly."],1],["cp10",0,1,"A transactional database on a cloud VM needs consistent low-latency disk performance with a guaranteed number of I/O operations per second. What should be provisioned?",["A block volume with provisioned IOPS","An object storage bucket mounted with a FUSE driver","A cold file share tier","An archive storage tier with expedited retrieval"],0,"Block volumes with provisioned IOPS give predictable latency and throughput for databases. Object storage mounted through a driver and cold or archive tiers are far too slow for transactional I/O.","Domain 1: storage performance",[null,"Mounting a bucket with FUSE translates file operations into HTTP object calls, giving high and unpredictable latency unsuitable for a transactional database.","A cold file share tier is optimized for low storage cost on infrequently accessed files, not guaranteed low-latency IOPS.","Archive storage must be restored before it can be read, and even expedited retrieval takes minutes; it cannot serve live database I/O."],2],["cp11",0,1,"Audit logs must be kept for seven years and are almost never read; retrieving them within 12 hours is acceptable. Which storage choice minimizes cost?",["Archive object storage tier","Provisioned IOPS block volumes","Standard object storage tier","Premium file share"],0,"Archive tiers have the lowest storage price in exchange for slow, paid retrieval, which suits long retention with rare access. Standard tier costs more per GB, and block or premium file storage is far more expensive.","Domain 1: storage tiers",[null,"Provisioned IOPS block volumes are premium, performance-priced storage, the opposite of what rarely read logs need.","The standard object tier is priced for frequent access, so seven years of rarely read logs would cost much more than an archive tier.","A premium file share uses high-performance storage billed at a high rate, which wastes money on data that is almost never read."],1],["cp12",0,1,"Each time a customer uploads a receipt image, a small piece of code must create a thumbnail. Uploads are unpredictable, and the team wants to pay nothing when idle. Which approach fits?",["A reserved VM that polls the bucket every minute","A function triggered by the storage upload event","A container cluster with three always-on nodes","A scheduled nightly batch job on a spot instance"],1,"Serverless functions run in response to events and bill per invocation, so idle time costs nothing. Always-on VMs or nodes cost money while idle, and a nightly batch delays the thumbnail.","Domain 1: serverless",["A reserved VM bills around the clock even when idle, and polling every minute adds delay and wasted work.",null,"An always-on three-node container cluster costs money when there are no uploads, which the team wants to avoid.","A nightly batch means thumbnails appear hours late, and the job still does not respond to each upload as it happens."],2],["cp13",0,1,"Which statement correctly describes how containers differ from virtual machines?",["Containers share the host operating system kernel, while each VM runs its own guest OS","Containers each include a full guest OS and a hypervisor","Containers cannot run on cloud infrastructure, only on physical servers","Containers provide stronger isolation than VMs because they use hardware virtualization"],0,"Containers package an application and its dependencies but share the host kernel, making them lighter and faster to start. VMs virtualize hardware and each runs a complete OS, which gives stronger isolation.","Domain 1: containers",[null,"That describes virtual machines; containers do not include a full guest OS or a hypervisor, they share the host kernel.","Containers run widely on cloud infrastructure, including managed container and Kubernetes services.","It is the reverse: VMs use hardware virtualization and offer stronger isolation, while containers only use OS-level isolation."],1],["cp14",0,1,"An online store's checkout service is overloaded during sales, while its catalog and review services are idle. The team splits the monolith into separate services. Which benefit does this give most directly?",["Fewer network calls between components","A single shared database schema for all features","Each service can be scaled independently","Simpler end-to-end debugging"],2,"Microservices let each component scale, deploy and fail on its own, so checkout can scale without scaling everything. They usually increase network calls and make debugging harder, not simpler.","Domain 1: microservices",["Splitting a monolith usually adds network calls, because in-process function calls become calls between services.","Microservices typically give each service its own data store; a single shared schema is a monolith trait that couples services together.",null,"Debugging across many services is harder, since a request crosses several processes and needs tracing to follow."],2],["cp15",0,1,"Instances in a private subnet must download OS updates from the internet but must never accept inbound connections from it. What should be added?",["A NAT gateway in a public subnet, with a default route from the private subnet","Public IP addresses on each instance","An internet gateway route directly from the private subnet","A VPC peering connection to another private network"],0,"A NAT gateway lets private instances start outbound connections while blocking unsolicited inbound ones. Routing the private subnet straight to an internet gateway or adding public IPs would expose the instances.","Domain 1: virtual networks",[null,"A public IP on each instance makes them directly reachable from the internet, which violates the no-inbound requirement.","Routing a private subnet straight to an internet gateway effectively makes it public and still needs public IPs, exposing the instances.","Peering connects two private networks to each other; it gives no path to the internet for downloading updates."],2],["cp16",0,1,"A company moves large nightly data sets between its data center and the cloud and needs consistent latency and bandwidth that do not travel over the public internet. Which connection should it use?",["A site-to-site IPsec VPN over broadband","A dedicated private interconnect such as Direct Connect, ExpressRoute or Cloud Interconnect","A client VPN on the backup server","A public endpoint protected by TLS"],1,"Dedicated interconnects are private circuits into the provider with predictable performance. A site-to-site VPN is encrypted but still rides the internet, so latency and throughput vary.","Domain 1: hybrid connectivity",["An IPsec VPN encrypts traffic but still travels over the public internet, so latency and bandwidth vary.",null,"A client VPN connects individual devices, still rides the internet and is not built for bulk site-to-cloud transfers.","TLS encrypts data in transit, but a public endpoint still uses the internet with its variable performance."],3],["cp17",0,1,"A web application needs requests for /api/* sent to one group of servers and /images/* to another, based on the URL path. Which component provides this?",["A network (layer 4) load balancer","DNS round robin across both server groups","An application (layer 7) load balancer","A NAT gateway"],2,"Layer 7 load balancers read HTTP details such as path and host header and route accordingly. Layer 4 balancers only see IP addresses and ports, and DNS round robin cannot inspect paths.","Domain 1: load balancing",["A layer 4 load balancer routes on IP address and port only and cannot see the HTTP URL path.","DNS round robin just hands out different IP addresses per lookup; DNS never sees the URL path of a request.",null,"A NAT gateway translates outbound private addresses for internet access; it does not distribute or route inbound HTTP requests."],1],["cp18",0,1,"Users in Asia complain that a site hosted in a European region loads its images and scripts slowly. The content rarely changes. What is the most cost-effective improvement?",["Move the whole application to a larger, compute-optimized instance size","Add a second database read replica in Europe","Serve static content through a content delivery network","Increase the load balancer idle timeout"],2,"A CDN caches static files at edge locations near users, cutting latency. A bigger instance or more replicas in Europe does nothing for the distance to Asia.","Domain 1: content delivery",["A bigger, compute-optimized instance does not shorten the physical distance between Asia and Europe, which causes the latency.","A database read replica helps with query load, but the slowness is static files crossing continents, not the database.",null,"The load balancer idle timeout only controls how long idle connections stay open; it does not speed up delivery to distant users."],2],["cp19",0,1,"A company's ERP servers run 24 hours a day at steady load and will stay in the cloud for at least three years. Which pricing model gives the largest savings?",["Reserved instances or committed use discounts","On-demand instances","Spot instances","Pay-per-invocation pricing by rewriting the ERP as serverless functions"],0,"Committing to one or three years of steady usage earns large discounts. Spot capacity can be reclaimed, which is not acceptable for an ERP, and on-demand is the most expensive for constant use.","Domain 1: cost models",[null,"On-demand pricing is the most expensive option for a constant 24/7 workload because it carries no commitment discount.","Spot capacity can be reclaimed at short notice, which is unacceptable for an always-on ERP system.","Rewriting an ERP as functions is a huge, risky project, and steady 24/7 load is often more expensive per invocation than reserved VMs."],1],["cp20",0,1,"A research lab runs thousands of simulation jobs that checkpoint their progress and can restart if interrupted. It wants the lowest compute cost. Which option fits?",["Spot or preemptible instances","Dedicated hosts","Three-year reserved instances","On-demand instances in two regions"],0,"Spot capacity is deeply discounted but can be reclaimed at short notice, which is fine for fault-tolerant, checkpointed batch work. Dedicated hosts and on-demand cost more, and reservations suit steady, always-on load.","Domain 1: cost models",[null,"Dedicated hosts are among the most expensive options and address licensing or isolation, not lowest cost for interruptible jobs.","Three-year reservations suit steady always-on usage; they commit spend for years for work that can simply use cheap spare capacity.","On-demand instances cost full price, and running them in two regions adds cost without benefit for restartable batch jobs."],2],["cp21",0,1,"Finance wants each department's monthly cloud spend reported back to it without actually moving money between cost centers. What should be implemented?",["Showback reports built on consistent resource tags such as department and project","Chargeback using separate credit cards per team","A single shared account with no tags","Reserved instances purchased by each department"],0,"Showback reports costs to teams for awareness, and consistent tags make the costs attributable. Chargeback actually bills the departments, which finance said it does not want.","Domain 1: cost management",[null,"Chargeback actually bills departments, moving money between cost centers, which finance explicitly does not want.","One shared account with no tags makes it impossible to attribute spend to departments at all.","Reserved instances lower costs for steady workloads but do nothing to report spend back to departments."],2],["cp22",0,2,"A team runs two identical production environments. The new release is deployed to the idle one, tested, and then all traffic is switched to it at the load balancer, with the old one kept for instant rollback. Which strategy is this?",["Canary","Rolling","Blue-green","In-place upgrade of each server"],2,"Blue-green keeps two full environments and switches traffic between them, so rollback means switching back. Canary moves a small percentage of users first, and rolling updates instances in batches.","Domain 2: deployment strategies",["A canary sends a small percentage of real traffic to the new version first, rather than switching everything between two full environments.","A rolling deployment updates instances in batches within one environment instead of flipping traffic to a separate full copy.",null,"An in-place upgrade changes the existing servers directly, so there is no idle second environment to switch back to."],1],["cp23",0,2,"A company releases a new version to 5% of users, watches error rates and latency for an hour, and then gradually increases the share if the metrics stay healthy. Which strategy is this?",["Blue-green","Canary","Big bang","Recreate"],1,"A canary release exposes a small slice of real traffic first and widens it as confidence grows. Blue-green switches all traffic at once.","Domain 2: deployment strategies",["Blue-green switches all traffic from one full environment to the other at once, not a gradual 5% start.",null,"A big bang release pushes the new version to everyone at the same moment, with no small initial slice.","The recreate strategy stops the old version completely and then starts the new one, causing downtime and no gradual exposure."],1],["cp24",0,2,"An autoscaling group of 12 web servers must be updated without a second full environment and without dropping below 75% capacity. Which approach fits?",["Blue-green deployment","Terminate all instances and relaunch with the new image","A canary release to a separate region","A rolling deployment in batches of three instances"],3,"Rolling updates replace instances a batch at a time, so replacing three of twelve keeps 75% serving. Blue-green would need a second full environment, and terminating everything causes downtime.","Domain 2: deployment strategies",["Blue-green requires a second full environment, which the scenario explicitly rules out.","Terminating all instances drops capacity to zero and causes an outage until the new ones are ready.","A canary in a separate region needs extra infrastructure there and does not describe how to update the existing twelve servers.",null],3],["cp25",0,2,"A data center lease ends in four months. The company must move 200 VMs to the cloud as quickly as possible, with no code changes, and optimize later. Which migration strategy fits?",["Refactor into microservices","Rehost (lift and shift)","Repurchase as SaaS","Retire all 200 servers"],1,"Rehosting moves VMs largely as they are, which is fastest under a deadline. Refactoring takes much longer, and repurchasing or retiring only fits some applications.","Domain 2: migration strategies",["Refactoring into microservices requires major code changes and takes far longer than four months for 200 VMs.",null,"Repurchasing replaces applications with SaaS products; it cannot quickly cover 200 varied VMs and changes the software.","Retiring means decommissioning systems that are no longer needed; the company still needs these workloads running."],1],["cp26",0,2,"During migration, a team moves its self-managed SQL Server VM to the provider's managed SQL database service, with only connection-string changes to the application. Which strategy is this?",["Rehost","Replatform","Retain","Refactor (re-architect)"],1,"Replatforming makes small optimizations, such as adopting a managed database, without redesigning the application. Refactoring would change the application architecture itself.","Domain 2: migration strategies",["Rehosting would move the SQL Server VM as-is to a cloud VM, not switch to a managed database service.",null,"Retain means leaving the workload where it is for now, but the database is actually being moved.","Refactoring redesigns the application architecture itself, while here only the connection string changes."],1],["cp27",0,2,"A company decides to stop running its on-premises customer relationship software and moves its users to a subscription CRM service instead. Which migration strategy is this?",["Replatform","Retain","Rehost","Repurchase"],3,"Repurchasing replaces an application with a different product, usually SaaS. Rehosting and replatforming move the same application.","Domain 2: migration strategies",["Replatforming moves the same application with small optimizations; here the company drops its software for a different product.","Retain means keeping the application where it is, but the on-premises CRM is being shut down.","Rehosting lifts the same application onto cloud VMs unchanged, not replacing it with a subscription service.",null],3],["cp28",0,2,"A company must move 400 TB of archive data to the cloud over a 200 Mbps internet link that is already busy. Which approach is most practical?",["Order an offline data transfer appliance from the provider","Use a site-to-site VPN to improve throughput","Compress the data and upload it over the existing link","Copy the data with many parallel HTTPS upload streams from several servers"],0,"At 200 Mbps, 400 TB would take more than half a year even with the link fully free, so shipping a transfer appliance is faster. A VPN adds encryption, not bandwidth, and parallel uploads cannot exceed the link.","Domain 2: data transfer",[null,"A VPN adds encryption on top of the same internet link; it cannot increase the available bandwidth.","Compression may shrink the data somewhat, but already-compressed archives gain little and months of transfer would remain on a busy link.","Parallel streams cannot exceed the 200 Mbps link capacity, and they would saturate a link that is already busy."],2],["cp29",0,2,"Before a migration, the team uses a discovery tool and finds that a reporting server makes nightly calls to the payroll database. Why does this matter for planning?",["The dependent servers should be moved in the same migration wave or connectivity must be planned","It means the payroll database must be retired","It proves the reporting server should be rehosted as SaaS","It shows the network needs larger subnets"],0,"Dependency mapping groups tightly linked systems into the same wave, or ensures hybrid connectivity during the move, so nothing breaks at cutover.","Domain 2: migration planning",[null,"A dependency shows the database is in use, which argues for keeping it, not retiring it.","SaaS is a repurchase option for replacing software; a dependency between servers says nothing about rehosting as SaaS.","Nightly calls between two servers reveal an application dependency, not a shortage of IP addresses in the subnets."],3],["cp30",0,2,"New web servers take 40 minutes to become ready because each one installs packages and agents at boot. The team wants them ready in a few minutes with identical settings. What should it use?",["A longer autoscaling cooldown period","A pre-built golden image with the software already installed","Manual configuration of each new server by the operations team using a written checklist","A larger instance type"],1,"A golden image bakes in software, agents and hardening, so instances boot ready and consistent. A bigger instance or longer cooldown does not remove the install time.","Domain 2: images and templates",["A cooldown period only controls how long autoscaling waits between actions; it does not make each server boot faster.",null,"Manual configuration is slow and error-prone and would make servers less consistent, the opposite of the goal.","A larger instance may install packages slightly faster but still runs the same 40-minute boot-time setup."],2],["cp31",0,2,"An in-memory caching cluster keeps running out of RAM while its CPUs sit at 10%. Which instance family should be chosen when it is redeployed?",["Memory-optimized","Compute-optimized","Storage-optimized","GPU-accelerated"],0,"Memory-optimized families offer a high RAM-to-vCPU ratio, which suits caches and in-memory databases. Compute-optimized would add CPU the workload does not need.","Domain 2: provisioning compute",[null,"Compute-optimized instances have a high vCPU-to-RAM ratio, adding CPU the cache does not need while still running short of memory.","Storage-optimized instances focus on fast local disk throughput, not on the large RAM an in-memory cache requires.","GPU-accelerated instances add graphics or ML accelerators, which an in-memory cache cannot use, at a high price."],1],["cp32",0,2,"A private cloud administrator allocated 40 TB of thin-provisioned volumes on a 20 TB storage pool. What is the main risk to monitor?",["Volumes will be slower because space is pre-zeroed","Thin provisioning disables encryption at rest","Snapshots cannot be taken of thin volumes","The pool can fill up as volumes grow, causing write failures for every volume on it"],3,"Thin provisioning allocates space on write, so overcommitting is allowed, but if real usage reaches the pool's capacity, writes fail. Pre-zeroing is a thick-provisioning behavior.","Domain 2: provisioning storage",["Pre-zeroing space is a thick-provisioning (eager-zeroed) behavior; thin volumes allocate on write rather than zeroing up front.","Thin provisioning is a space allocation method and has no effect on whether encryption at rest can be used.","Thin-provisioned volumes can be snapshotted normally; snapshots are not the risk created by overcommitting the pool.",null],3],["cp33",0,2,"A managed relational database is overloaded by reporting queries, while writes from the main application are light. Which change best reduces the load on the primary?",["Enable a multi-AZ standby replica and point the reporting tool at the standby's endpoint","Increase the backup retention period","Switch the database to an archive storage tier","Create a read replica and send reporting queries to it"],3,"Read replicas serve read-only traffic such as reports, taking load off the primary. In many services the multi-AZ standby only takes over on failover and cannot serve reads.","Domain 2: managed services",["In many managed database services the multi-AZ standby is passive and only takes over on failover, so it cannot serve the reporting queries.","Backup retention controls how long backups are kept; it does nothing to reduce query load on the primary.","An archive tier is for rarely accessed data and would make an active database far slower, not relieve it.",null],3],["cp34",0,2,"A team defines its network, VMs and database in version-controlled templates and deploys identical development, staging and production environments from them. What is the main benefit?",["It removes the need for any testing","It guarantees the cloud bill will fall","Environments are repeatable and consistent, with changes reviewed in code","It replaces the need for monitoring"],2,"Infrastructure as code makes environments repeatable and reviewable, reducing configuration drift. It does not remove the need for testing or monitoring, and it does not by itself cut costs.","Domain 2: infrastructure as code",["IaC makes environments consistent, but code and infrastructure still need testing; templates can contain bugs too.","IaC does not by itself lower spending; it can just as easily deploy expensive resources consistently.",null,"Consistently deployed environments still need monitoring to detect failures, performance issues and security events."],2],["cp35",0,2,"A company's policy says running servers are never patched or changed by hand; instead, a new image is built and the servers are replaced. What is this practice called?",["Configuration drift","In-place upgrading with a configuration tool","Immutable infrastructure","Vertical scaling"],2,"Immutable infrastructure replaces servers with new ones built from updated images rather than modifying them. In-place upgrades change running systems, and drift is what this practice prevents.","Domain 2: immutable infrastructure",["Configuration drift is the problem of servers slowly diverging from their intended state, which this practice is designed to prevent.","In-place upgrading modifies running servers, which is exactly what the policy forbids.",null,"Vertical scaling means resizing a server to more or less CPU and memory, unrelated to how changes are applied."],1],["cp36",0,2,"Right after a production release, the team runs a short automated script that signs in, adds an item to the cart and loads the order page. What is this test called?",["Load test","Smoke test","Penetration test","User acceptance test"],1,"A smoke test quickly checks that the most important functions work after deployment. Load tests measure capacity, penetration tests look for vulnerabilities, and UAT is business sign-off before release.","Domain 2: post-deployment validation",["A load test measures how the system behaves under heavy traffic, not a quick check of key functions after a release.",null,"A penetration test is a security assessment that tries to exploit vulnerabilities, not a post-deploy functionality check.","User acceptance testing is business users confirming requirements before release, not an automated script run just after deployment."],1],["cp37",0,2,"A team wants to run containers across several nodes, with failed containers restarted automatically and replicas spread across zones, but without managing the control plane. What should it deploy?",["A managed Kubernetes service","A single large VM running Docker","A serverless function per container","A file share for container images"],0,"Managed Kubernetes provides scheduling, self-healing and replica management while the provider runs the control plane. A single VM has no cross-node scheduling or zone spread.","Domain 2: managed services",[null,"A single VM running Docker has no cross-node scheduling, no zone spread and becomes a single point of failure.","Serverless functions run short event-driven code; they do not orchestrate long-running container replicas across nodes.","A file share can store files but provides no scheduling, self-healing or replica management for containers."],2],["cp38",0,2,"A migration cutover is complete. Which step should come next before the old environment is shut down?",["Validate functionality and performance against the pre-migration baseline","Delete all backups from the source system","Disable monitoring to avoid false alerts","Remove DNS records for the new environment"],0,"Post-migration validation compares the new system with the baseline and confirms it works before decommissioning the source. Deleting backups or disabling monitoring removes your safety net.","Domain 2: migration validation",[null,"Deleting source backups removes the ability to roll back if the new environment turns out to have problems.","Disabling monitoring right after cutover hides exactly the problems the team most needs to catch.","Removing DNS records for the new environment would cut users off from the system that was just migrated."],2],["cp39",0,3,"A stateless web tier behind a load balancer slows down during traffic spikes. The team wants to add capacity without any downtime. What should it do?",["Scale vertically by resizing each instance to the next larger size one at a time","Move the web tier to an archive storage tier","Scale horizontally by adding instances through autoscaling","Increase the DNS TTL"],2,"Horizontal scaling adds instances behind the load balancer without interrupting service, which suits stateless tiers. Resizing an instance usually needs a restart.","Domain 3: scaling",["Resizing an instance usually requires a stop and restart, and vertical scaling has an upper limit, so it is not the zero-downtime way to add capacity.","Archive storage is for rarely read data; a web tier cannot run from it, and it would add no compute capacity.",null,"A longer DNS TTL only affects how long resolvers cache records; it adds no capacity to the web tier."],2],["cp40",0,3,"A legacy application cannot run on more than one server, and it is now CPU-bound. Which scaling option is available?",["Horizontal scaling with an autoscaling group","Adding a content delivery network","Adding read replicas to the web tier","Vertical scaling to a larger instance size during a maintenance window"],3,"If the app cannot be distributed, the only option is to scale up to a bigger instance, which usually needs a restart. Horizontal scaling requires the app to run on several servers.","Domain 3: scaling",["Horizontal scaling adds more servers, but this application cannot run on more than one server.","A CDN offloads static content delivery, but it will not relieve a CPU-bound application server.","Read replicas are a database feature for offloading reads; they do not apply to a CPU-bound application tier.",null],3],["cp41",0,3,"A payroll system's load rises sharply every Monday at 8:00, and reactive scaling adds servers too late. Which autoscaling approach fixes this?",["Removing the load balancer health check","A longer cooldown on the CPU policy","A higher CPU threshold for scaling out, so that brief spikes are ignored","Scheduled scaling that adds capacity shortly before 8:00 on Mondays"],3,"Scheduled scaling prepares capacity ahead of a known, predictable peak. A longer cooldown or higher threshold would make the reaction even slower.","Domain 3: autoscaling",["Removing the health check stops the load balancer from detecting failed servers; it does not add capacity earlier.","A longer cooldown makes autoscaling wait even longer between actions, so capacity arrives later still.","Raising the scale-out threshold means the policy reacts even later to the Monday surge.",null],3],["cp42",0,3,"Users report that some checkout requests take eight seconds, and a request passes through six microservices. Which tool best shows which service is adding the delay?",["CPU metrics for each host","Distributed tracing","A daily log archive","An uptime check on the home page"],1,"Distributed tracing follows a single request across services and shows the time spent in each. Host CPU metrics and uptime checks cannot attribute latency to a specific hop.","Domain 3: observability",["Host CPU metrics show resource usage per machine, but cannot attribute latency of one request to a specific service hop.",null,"An archived daily log is not correlated per request and is hard to use for tracking latency across six services.","An uptime check only confirms the home page responds; it says nothing about which checkout service is slow."],1],["cp43",0,3,"The on-call engineer receives hundreds of CPU alerts a week, and most clear on their own within a minute. What is the best improvement?",["Tune thresholds against a baseline and require the condition to persist for several minutes","Send every alert to the whole team instead","Disable CPU alerting entirely","Lower the CPU threshold to catch problems earlier"],0,"Alert fatigue is reduced by using baselines and a sustained duration, so only meaningful conditions page someone. Disabling alerts hides real problems, and lowering the threshold creates even more noise.","Domain 3: alerting",[null,"Sending every alert to the whole team spreads the same noise to more people and worsens alert fatigue.","Disabling CPU alerting entirely hides real, sustained CPU problems along with the noise.","Lowering the threshold would fire even more alerts, making the noise problem worse."],2],["cp44",0,3,"Security requires that logs from 300 VMs and several managed services be searchable in one place and kept for one year, even if an instance is deleted. What should be implemented?",["Local log rotation on each VM with a one-year rotation schedule configured","Screenshots of dashboards stored weekly","Centralized log aggregation with a retention policy","Larger boot disks on each VM"],2,"Shipping logs to a central service keeps them searchable and retained independently of the source instance. Local logs vanish when the VM is deleted.","Domain 3: logging",["Local logs stay on each VM and disappear when an instance is deleted, and they are not searchable in one place.","Dashboard screenshots capture pictures of metrics, not searchable log data, and cannot meet a retention requirement.",null,"Bigger boot disks only store more logs locally; they are still lost when the VM is deleted and not centrally searchable."],2],["cp45",0,3,"A company takes a full backup every Sunday and differential backups every other night. The server fails on Thursday afternoon. Which backups are needed to restore it to Wednesday night's state?",["Sunday's full and every differential from Monday to Wednesday","Only Wednesday's differential","Sunday's full and Wednesday's differential","Every full backup from the last month"],2,"A differential holds all changes since the last full backup, so you need the full plus the latest differential. Needing every nightly backup describes an incremental scheme.","Domain 3: backup types",["Each differential already contains all changes since Sunday, so Monday's and Tuesday's are redundant; applying every nightly backup describes incremental restores.","A differential only contains changes since the last full, so it cannot restore the server without Sunday's full backup.",null,"Older full backups are unnecessary; only the most recent full plus the latest differential are needed."],1],["cp46",0,3,"After ransomware hit a cloud account, the attacker also deleted every snapshot stored in that same account. Which change best prevents this next time?",["Take snapshots more frequently in the same account","Store backups in a separate account with immutable retention and restricted delete rights","Encrypt snapshots with the provider-managed key","Keep only one snapshot to reduce cost"],1,"Isolated, immutable backups in a separate security boundary cannot be deleted by an attacker who compromises the production account. More frequent snapshots in the same account are just as exposed.","Domain 3: backup protection",["More snapshots in the same account are just as exposed; an attacker with account access can delete them all again.",null,"Encryption protects confidentiality, but an attacker with account access can still delete encrypted snapshots.","Keeping a single snapshot reduces recovery options and does nothing to stop an attacker deleting it."],2],["cp47",0,3,"The backup job has reported success every night for a year, but no one has ever restored from it. What is the most important next step?",["Perform a scheduled test restore and verify the data and the recovery time","Switch the backup schedule to weekly","Increase the retention period to five years","Move the backups to a cheaper archive tier and extend their retention to cover a full audit cycle"],0,"Only a restore test proves backups are usable and shows whether the RTO can be met. A successful job status does not confirm the data can actually be recovered.","Domain 3: restore testing",[null,"Running backups less often increases potential data loss and still does not prove the backups can be restored.","Longer retention just keeps more backups that may be unusable; it does not verify recoverability.","Moving backups to an archive tier lowers cost but slows restores and still does not prove the data is recoverable."],2],["cp48",0,3,"A company must apply a critical OS patch to 50 web servers behind a load balancer without causing an outage. What is the best approach?",["Patch all servers at once during peak hours","Patch only the servers that report errors","Wait for the next major OS release instead","Patch in small batches, removing each batch from the load balancer, then verify before continuing"],3,"Patching in batches while the rest keep serving traffic avoids downtime, and verifying between batches limits the impact of a bad patch.","Domain 3: patch management",["Patching everything at once during peak hours risks a full outage and exposes all users if the patch fails.","Critical patches fix vulnerabilities on all servers, not just those currently showing errors; unpatched servers stay exposed.","Waiting for the next major release leaves a known critical vulnerability open for a long time.",null],3],["cp49",0,3,"The provider announces that the managed database engine version a company uses will reach end of support in six months and will then be upgraded automatically. What should the team do?",["Ignore it, since keeping managed services on supported versions is entirely the provider's responsibility","Disable automatic backups to block the upgrade","Plan, test and schedule the upgrade in a maintenance window before the deadline","Move the database to a larger instance to avoid the upgrade"],2,"Deprecations should be handled proactively: test the application on the new version and upgrade on your schedule rather than accept an unplanned forced upgrade. Instance size has no effect on version support.","Domain 3: lifecycle management",["Under shared responsibility the provider forces the upgrade, but testing application compatibility and choosing the timing are the customer's job.","Disabling backups does not block the upgrade and removes the ability to recover if something goes wrong.",null,"Instance size has no bearing on engine version support, so a larger instance would still be upgraded."],2],["cp50",0,3,"Monitoring shows an 8-vCPU application server has averaged 4% CPU and 15% memory use for three months. What should the administrator recommend?",["Enable vertical autoscaling to a larger size","Add a second identical server for redundancy of capacity","Buy a three-year reservation for the current size","Right-size it to a smaller instance type"],3,"Consistently low utilization means the instance is oversized; right-sizing cuts cost. Reserving the oversized instance would lock in the waste.","Domain 3: right-sizing",["The server is heavily underused, so moving to a larger size would increase waste and cost.","Adding a second identical server doubles cost for capacity that is already mostly unused; this is not a redundancy question.","Reserving the current oversized instance for three years locks in the waste instead of eliminating it.",null],2],["cp51",0,3,"A service has a 99.9% monthly availability SLA. Roughly how much downtime does this allow in a 30-day month?",["About 4.3 minutes","About 7 hours","About 1 minute","About 43 minutes"],3,"0.1% of 43,200 minutes is about 43 minutes. About 4 minutes corresponds to 99.99%.","Domain 3: SLAs",["About 4.3 minutes per month corresponds to 99.99% availability, not 99.9%.","About 7 hours per month corresponds to roughly 99% availability, ten times more downtime than 99.9% allows.","About 1 minute per month is closer to 99.998% availability, far stricter than a 99.9% SLA.",null],1],["cp52",0,3,"Objects in a log bucket are read often for the first 30 days, occasionally for a year and then never, but must be kept for seven years. What should be configured?",["A larger standard-tier bucket","Manual monthly moves between tiers by an administrator, followed by manual deletion once the logs are seven years old","Versioning with no expiration","A lifecycle policy that moves objects to cooler tiers over time and deletes them after seven years"],3,"Lifecycle policies automatically transition objects to cheaper tiers as access drops and expire them at the end of retention. Manual moves are error-prone, and versioning alone increases storage.","Domain 3: storage lifecycle",["A larger standard-tier bucket keeps everything at the most expensive tier and never deletes data after seven years.","Manual monthly moves and deletions are error-prone and labor-intensive when a lifecycle policy can automate both.","Versioning with no expiration keeps every object version forever, increasing storage and never enforcing the seven-year limit.",null],3],["cp53",0,3,"A company wants to know that its sign-in page works for users in three continents before customers report a problem. What should it set up?",["Host-level disk metrics","A monthly penetration test of the sign-in page by an external security firm","Synthetic monitoring that runs scripted sign-ins from several locations","A longer log retention period"],2,"Synthetic monitors run scripted user journeys from different regions on a schedule and alert when they fail. Disk metrics and log retention do not test the user experience.","Domain 3: observability",["Host disk metrics show server resource usage, not whether users can actually sign in from different continents.","A monthly penetration test looks for security weaknesses; it does not continuously check that sign-in works.",null,"Keeping logs longer helps investigate after the fact but does not proactively detect a broken sign-in page."],2],["cp54",0,4,"An application on a cloud VM needs to read files from an object storage bucket. A developer proposes putting an access key in the application's configuration file. What is the better approach?",["Attach an instance role or managed identity with read-only permission to that bucket","Commit the key to a private repository","Store the key in an environment variable on the VM and rotate it by hand every ninety days","Make the bucket public and restrict it by obscurity"],0,"Workload identities give the VM short-lived credentials automatically, with least-privilege access and no stored secrets. Environment variables and repositories still hold long-lived keys that can leak.","Domain 4: workload identity",[null,"Committing a key to any repository, even a private one, spreads a long-lived secret to everyone with repo access and its history.","An environment variable still holds a long-lived key on the VM that can leak, and manual rotation is error-prone.","A public bucket is readable by anyone on the internet; obscurity is not access control."],1],["cp55",0,4,"A new cloud account was opened with the root (owner) user, which the team still uses for everyday tasks. What should be done first?",["Enable MFA on the root user, create named admin accounts for daily work and lock the root credentials away","Share the root password with the whole team for convenience","Delete the root user","Create access keys for the root user for automation"],0,"The root user should be protected with MFA and used only for the few tasks that require it. It generally cannot be deleted, and root access keys are a major risk.","Domain 4: identity",[null,"Sharing the root password destroys accountability and exposes the most powerful credential to everyone.","The root user generally cannot be deleted, and some account tasks still require it.","Root access keys give unrestricted programmatic access and are a major risk; automation should use scoped roles."],1],["cp56",0,4,"A company wants employees to sign in to the cloud console with their existing corporate directory accounts, so that disabling an employee in the directory removes cloud access. What should be configured?",["Separate cloud user accounts whose passwords are kept in sync with the directory by a nightly script","Federation with the corporate identity provider using SAML or OpenID Connect","A shared administrator account with MFA","Access keys for every employee"],1,"Federation lets the corporate IdP authenticate users, so access is removed centrally when the account is disabled. Separate cloud accounts must be removed by hand and easily get missed.","Domain 4: federation and SSO",["Separate cloud accounts synced by script still exist independently, so disabling someone in the directory may not remove cloud access.",null,"A shared admin account breaks accountability and cannot be tied to individual employee status in the directory.","Access keys are long-lived programmatic credentials that are not removed when the directory account is disabled."],2],["cp57",0,4,"A regulator requires that the company control, rotate and be able to disable the keys that encrypt its stored data, with audit logs of every key use. What should be used?",["Provider-managed default encryption keys, which are turned on automatically for all storage","Customer-managed keys in the provider's key management service","TLS 1.3 on all endpoints","Hashing all stored files"],1,"Customer-managed keys in a KMS give control over key policy, rotation and disabling, with usage logged. Default provider keys give less control, TLS protects data in transit and hashing is not reversible encryption.","Domain 4: key management",["Provider-managed default keys are controlled and rotated by the provider, so the company cannot set key policy or disable them itself.",null,"TLS protects data in transit between endpoints, not the keys encrypting data at rest.","Hashing is one-way and cannot be reversed to read data, so it is not encryption with controllable keys."],2],["cp58",0,4,"A web server's security group allows inbound TCP 443 from anywhere. The subnet's network ACL allows inbound 443 but has no outbound rules beyond the default deny. Clients cannot load the site. Why?",["Security groups are stateless, so they need an outbound rule","Network ACLs are stateless, so return traffic to client ephemeral ports needs an outbound allow rule","The load balancer must use port 80","TLS certificates cannot be used behind a network ACL"],1,"NACLs do not track connections, so responses to ephemeral ports must be allowed explicitly. Security groups are stateful and permit return traffic automatically.","Domain 4: network security controls",["Security groups are stateful and automatically allow return traffic, so they do not need an outbound rule for responses.",null,"HTTPS on port 443 is perfectly normal behind a load balancer; switching to port 80 would not fix a blocked return path.","Network ACLs filter at the IP and port level and have no effect on whether TLS certificates can be used."],2],["cp59",0,4,"Logs show attackers sending requests containing ' OR 1=1 -- to a public web application's login form. Which control can block these requests before they reach the app?",["A network ACL rule that denies inbound port 443 from unknown sources","A NAT gateway","A web application firewall with SQL injection rules","A larger load balancer"],2,"A WAF inspects HTTP requests and blocks common attack patterns such as SQL injection. Blocking port 443 would stop all legitimate traffic too.","Domain 4: WAF",["Denying port 443 from unknown sources would block legitimate customers too, and a NACL cannot inspect the request content.","A NAT gateway provides outbound internet access for private instances and does not inspect inbound HTTP requests.",null,"A larger load balancer adds capacity but passes malicious requests through unchanged."],1],["cp60",0,4,"A developer committed a database password to a Git repository. After rotating the password, how should the application get it in the future?",["Retrieve it at runtime from a secrets manager using the workload's identity","Hard-code it in the container image","Put it in the VM's user data script, which runs only once at first boot and is then hidden from users","Store it base64-encoded in the repository"],0,"A secrets manager stores, audits and rotates secrets, and the application fetches them at runtime with its own identity. Base64 is encoding, not encryption, and images and user data are easily read.","Domain 4: secrets management",[null,"Hard-coding the password in an image exposes it to anyone who can pull or inspect the image and requires rebuilding to rotate.","User data is not hidden: it is retrievable from the instance metadata service and through the console or API, so secrets there are easily read.","Base64 is only encoding, trivially reversed, so the password would be exposed in the repository again."],1],["cp61",0,4,"Privacy law requires that EU customer data never be stored outside the EU. How can a cloud administrator best enforce this?",["Ask developers to remember the rule","Encrypt data with TLS","Apply an organization-wide policy that denies resource creation outside approved EU regions, and check backup and replica locations","Use a CDN with global edge caching for all customer data so that EU users always get fast responses from the nearest edge location"],2,"A preventive policy blocks non-compliant regions, and backups, replicas and logs must also stay in approved locations. A global CDN could cache personal data outside the EU.","Domain 4: data sovereignty",["Relying on developers' memory is not an enforceable control; one mistake could place data outside the EU.","TLS encrypts data in transit but does nothing to control which region the data is stored in.",null,"A global CDN caches content at edge locations worldwide, which could store EU personal data outside the EU."],2],["cp62",0,4,"A team wants to stop container images with critical vulnerabilities from ever reaching production. Where should image scanning be added?",["Only in production, once a quarter","In the CI/CD pipeline, failing the build when critical findings exist, plus registry scanning","On developers' laptops only","After an incident is reported"],1,"Scanning in the pipeline stops vulnerable images before deployment, and registry scanning catches newly published CVEs in stored images. Quarterly or after-incident scans are too late.","Domain 4: vulnerability management",["Quarterly scans in production find vulnerable images only after they are already running, which is too late.",null,"Laptop-only scanning is optional and inconsistent, and images built in the pipeline would bypass it.","Scanning after an incident is reactive; the vulnerable image has already reached production."],2],["cp63",0,4,"Across 40 cloud accounts, the security team wants continuous detection of misconfigurations such as public storage buckets, open management ports and disabled logging. Which tool category fits?",["Configuration management database only","Endpoint antivirus","Cloud security posture management","A network tap"],2,"CSPM tools continuously evaluate cloud configurations against best practices and compliance rules. Antivirus runs on hosts and cannot see account-level misconfigurations.","Domain 4: CSPM",["A CMDB records assets and their relationships but does not continuously evaluate cloud settings for misconfigurations.","Endpoint antivirus scans files and processes on hosts and cannot see account-level settings like public buckets or disabled logging.",null,"A network tap copies packets for analysis; it cannot detect configuration issues such as disabled logging."],2],["cp64",0,4,"An attacker who compromises one web server should not be able to reach the payment service on other ports. Every workload should allow only the flows it needs. Which approach does this describe?",["A single flat subnet with one broad security group","Microsegmentation with least-privilege rules between workloads","Placing all servers in a public subnet","Opening all ports internally and filtering only at the perimeter"],1,"Microsegmentation, a key zero trust practice, limits east-west movement by allowing only required flows between workloads. A flat network trusts everything inside the perimeter.","Domain 4: zero trust",["A flat subnet with one broad security group lets any compromised workload reach every other workload.",null,"Public subnets expose servers to the internet and do nothing to restrict traffic between workloads.","Perimeter-only filtering trusts everything inside, so an attacker who gets in can move laterally to any port."],2],["cp65",0,4,"A cloud VM is sending traffic to a known command-and-control address. The security team wants to stop the activity while preserving evidence. What should it do first?",["Terminate the instance immediately and launch a clean replacement from the latest golden image","Reboot the instance to clear memory","Delete its logs to save storage","Move it to an isolation security group with no outbound access and snapshot its disks"],3,"Isolating the VM contains the threat while snapshots preserve disk evidence. Terminating or rebooting destroys volatile and possibly disk evidence.","Domain 4: incident response",["Terminating the instance destroys memory and possibly disk evidence needed for the investigation.","Rebooting clears volatile memory, wiping evidence such as running processes and network connections.","Deleting logs destroys evidence and hampers the investigation; saving storage is irrelevant during an incident.",null],3],["cp66",0,4,"A production database was deleted overnight, and management wants to know which identity did it and from which IP address. Where should the administrator look?",["The CDN cache statistics","CPU metrics from the database","The provider's API audit log, such as CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log or Cloud Audit Logs","The VM's boot diagnostics"],2,"Control-plane audit logs record who called which API, when and from where. Performance metrics do not identify users.","Domain 4: audit logging",["CDN cache statistics show content delivery hit rates, not which identity called a delete operation.","CPU metrics show resource usage and cannot identify who deleted the database or from where.",null,"Boot diagnostics show a VM's console output and startup state, not control-plane API calls against the database."],1],["cp67",0,4,"A company wants to detect and block employees uploading files that contain credit card numbers to cloud storage shared outside the company. Which control fits?",["Data loss prevention with content inspection","Autoscaling policies on the file-sharing servers","A read replica","Reserved capacity"],0,"DLP classifies content, such as card number patterns, and can alert on or block sharing it. The other options have nothing to do with data protection.","Domain 4: data protection",[null,"Autoscaling adjusts capacity to load; it does not inspect file content or stop sensitive data from being shared.","A read replica copies a database to serve read queries and has nothing to do with inspecting uploaded files.","Reserved capacity is a pricing commitment and provides no data protection or content inspection."],2],["cp68",0,4,"A new standard VM image must meet an industry-recognized secure configuration baseline before it is approved. Which reference is commonly used?",["An SLA from the provider","A CIS benchmark for that operating system","The provider's pricing calculator for that operating system","The OSI model"],1,"CIS benchmarks give detailed, consensus hardening settings for operating systems and cloud services. An SLA covers availability, not configuration.","Domain 4: hardening",["An SLA defines the provider's availability and support commitments, not secure configuration settings for an image.",null,"A pricing calculator estimates costs and contains no security hardening guidance.","The OSI model is a conceptual networking layer model, not a configuration baseline for hardening an OS."],1],["cp69",0,4,"Card payment data is replaced in the application database with random surrogate values, and only a separate secured vault can map them back to real card numbers. What is this technique?",["Tokenization","Hashing","Compression","Salted hashing"],0,"Tokenization swaps sensitive data for tokens with no mathematical link to the original, and the mapping lives in a secured vault. Hashing is one-way and cannot be mapped back.","Domain 4: data protection",[null,"Hashing is one-way and cannot be mapped back to the original card number, whereas here a vault can recover it.","Compression reduces data size and is fully reversible by anyone, so it provides no protection for card data.","A salted hash is still a one-way function; the salt only defeats precomputed lookups and does not allow recovering the value from a vault."],1],["cp70",0,4,"A company wants its VMs to reach a managed storage service without any traffic crossing the public internet, and wants the service's public endpoint blocked. What should be configured?",["A CDN in front of the storage service, with the storage account's firewall set to allow all networks","A public IP on each VM","An internet gateway with a larger bandwidth limit","A private endpoint (private link) for the storage service in the virtual network"],3,"A private endpoint gives the service a private IP inside the virtual network, so traffic stays on the provider backbone and public access can be disabled.","Domain 4: network security controls",["A CDN serves content over the public internet, and allowing all networks leaves the public endpoint wide open.","A public IP on each VM sends traffic out over the internet, the opposite of the requirement.","An internet gateway routes traffic to the public internet; more bandwidth does not keep it private.",null],3],["cp71",0,5,"Two engineers change the same Terraform module. The team wants every change reviewed and tested before it reaches the main branch. Which workflow fits?",["Everyone commits directly to main","Editing the files on a shared network drive","Emailing ZIP files of changes to a lead","Feature branches merged through pull requests with required review and passing checks"],3,"Feature branches and pull requests allow review and automated checks before merging. Committing straight to main skips both.","Domain 5: source control",["Committing directly to main skips review and automated checks, so bad changes land immediately.","A shared network drive has no version history, review or merge process and invites overwriting each other's work.","Emailing ZIP files is manual, has no automated testing and makes merging conflicting changes error-prone.",null],3],["cp72",0,5,"Every time code is pushed, a pipeline automatically compiles it, runs unit tests and produces a versioned artifact. Which practice is this?",["Configuration drift","Blue-green deployment strategy","Continuous integration","Chaos engineering"],2,"Continuous integration builds and tests every change automatically so problems are caught early. Blue-green is a release strategy, not a build practice.","Domain 5: CI/CD",["Configuration drift is when systems diverge from their intended state; it is a problem, not a build practice.","Blue-green is a release strategy that switches traffic between environments, not an automated build-and-test process.",null,"Chaos engineering deliberately injects failures into running systems to test resilience; it does not compile and test commits."],1],["cp73",0,5,"A pipeline automatically builds, tests and deploys every change to staging, but a release manager must click Approve before production. What does this describe?",["Continuous deployment","Continuous delivery","Manual deployment","Continuous integration only"],1,"Continuous delivery keeps software always releasable, with a manual gate to production. Continuous deployment would release to production automatically with no approval.","Domain 5: CI/CD",["Continuous deployment pushes every passing change to production automatically with no manual approval gate.",null,"The pipeline automates build, test and staging deployment; only the production release has a manual approval, so it is not manual deployment.","Continuous integration only covers building and testing code; this pipeline also deploys to staging and gates production."],1],["cp74",0,5,"An operations team wants to configure 200 Linux servers over SSH, without installing an agent on them, using YAML playbooks. Which tool fits?",["Puppet","Chef","CloudFormation","Ansible"],3,"Ansible is agentless, connects over SSH (or WinRM) and uses YAML playbooks. Puppet and Chef normally use agents, and CloudFormation provisions AWS resources rather than configuring servers.","Domain 5: configuration management",["Puppet normally uses an agent on each managed node and its own declarative language rather than YAML playbooks.","Chef normally uses an agent (chef-client) on each node and Ruby-based recipes rather than YAML playbooks.","CloudFormation provisions AWS resources from templates; it does not configure the software on existing Linux servers over SSH.",null],1],["cp75",0,5,"An engineer writes a file that states 'there should be three web servers and one load balancer', and the tool works out whether to create, change or delete resources. What kind of approach is this?",["Imperative scripting with ordered steps","Declarative infrastructure as code","Manual provisioning","Ad hoc CLI commands"],1,"Declarative IaC describes the desired end state and the tool calculates the steps. Imperative scripts spell out each step in order.","Domain 5: infrastructure as code",["Imperative scripts list the exact steps to run in order, rather than declaring an end state the tool reconciles.",null,"Manual provisioning means an engineer creates resources by hand in a console, not through a file the tool evaluates.","Ad hoc CLI commands perform individual actions one at a time and do not compute differences from a desired state."],2],["cp76",0,5,"A team wants its pipeline to start automatically whenever code is pushed to the repository, instead of polling every five minutes. What should be configured?",["A webhook from the repository to the pipeline service","A cron job on the build server that checks the repository every minute","A larger build agent","An SNMP trap"],0,"A webhook sends an HTTP request to the pipeline the moment the push event happens. A cron job is still polling on a schedule.","Domain 5: webhooks and APIs",[null,"A cron job that checks every minute is still polling on a schedule, not triggering on the push event itself.","A larger build agent makes builds run faster but does not change what starts the pipeline.","SNMP traps are network device notifications for monitoring, not a way for a code repository to trigger a pipeline."],2],["cp77",0,5,"A script must create a new resource through a provider's REST API. Which HTTP method is normally used to create it?",["GET","HEAD","DELETE","POST"],3,"POST is the standard method for creating a resource in REST APIs. GET and HEAD read without changing anything, and DELETE removes a resource.","Domain 5: APIs",["GET retrieves a resource and should not create or change anything on the server.","HEAD is like GET but returns only headers; it reads metadata and creates nothing.","DELETE removes an existing resource rather than creating a new one.",null],1],["cp78",0,5,"A bug appeared in production that never appeared in staging, because the image was rebuilt separately for each environment and pulled newer packages. What practice prevents this?",["Always deploy the 'latest' tag","Build once, tag it immutably, and promote the same image through each environment","Rebuild the image in every environment","Install the latest package versions when each container starts so that every environment is always current"],1,"Promoting one immutable build guarantees staging and production run identical artifacts. Rebuilding, 'latest' tags and install-at-start all let versions drift.","Domain 5: containers",["The 'latest' tag is mutable and moves to new builds, so different environments can pull different images.",null,"Rebuilding per environment is the very cause of the bug, since each build can pull different package versions.","Installing the latest packages at container start makes every environment potentially different and unpredictable."],2],["cp79",0,5,"A pipeline fails with a parse error in its YAML definition, although every key name is spelled correctly. What is the most likely cause?",["Too many comments","Using double quotes around a string","Inconsistent indentation","The file uses lowercase keys"],2,"YAML uses indentation to define structure, so a misaligned line changes or breaks the hierarchy. Quotes, comments and lowercase keys are all valid.","Domain 5: data formats",["Comments are ignored by YAML parsers, so having many of them cannot cause a parse error.","Double quotes are valid YAML string syntax and do not break parsing.",null,"Lowercase keys are completely valid in YAML; key case does not cause parse errors."],2],["cp80",0,6,"Users say an internal cloud application is slow. According to the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology, what should the administrator do first?",["Restart the application servers","Document the fix in the knowledge base","Implement a larger instance type","Identify the problem by gathering information, questioning users and checking recent changes"],3,"The first step is to identify the problem: gather details, find symptoms and review recent changes. Acting before knowing the cause can make things worse.","Domain 6: methodology",["Restarting servers is acting before understanding the problem, may hide the cause and can disrupt users.","Documenting the fix is the final step of the methodology, after the problem has been resolved.","Implementing a larger instance assumes a cause and a solution before the problem has been identified.",null],2],["cp81",0,6,"A VM in a private subnet cannot reach an update repository on the internet. A NAT gateway exists in the public subnet. Security groups allow outbound traffic. What should be checked next?",["The load balancer listener's TLS certificate and the security policy attached to it","The VM's disk IOPS","The CDN cache settings","The private subnet's route table for a default route to the NAT gateway"],3,"Without a 0.0.0.0/0 route pointing at the NAT gateway, private traffic has no path out. Disk performance and certificates do not affect outbound routing.","Domain 6: network troubleshooting",["The load balancer's TLS certificate affects inbound HTTPS to the application, not outbound traffic from a private VM.","Disk IOPS affects storage performance, not whether the VM can route packets to the internet.","CDN cache settings affect content delivery to users and play no part in a VM's outbound routing.",null],3],["cp82",0,6,"After a new database was deployed, the application tier gets connection timeouts on TCP 5432. The database is running and healthy. What is the most likely cause?",["The database's security group lacks an inbound rule for 5432 from the application tier","The database is using too much memory","The application uses the wrong HTTP method","The object storage bucket is private"],0,"A timeout with a healthy database usually means traffic is being dropped, and a missing inbound rule is the most common reason. Memory pressure would show errors or slowness, not dropped packets.","Domain 6: network troubleshooting",[null,"High memory use would cause slowness or errors, but the database is healthy and timeouts suggest packets are being dropped.","PostgreSQL on 5432 uses its own wire protocol, not HTTP, so HTTP methods have nothing to do with the connection.","Object storage bucket permissions do not affect TCP connections from the app tier to the database."],1],["cp83",0,6,"New instances launched by an autoscaling group are marked unhealthy and replaced every few minutes, although the application works when tested directly on port 8080. What is the most likely cause?",["The instances are too large","The load balancer health check is probing the wrong port or path","The autoscaling group has a maximum size set lower than its desired capacity","The instances have too much memory"],1,"If the health check targets a port or path the app does not serve, healthy instances fail checks and get replaced in a loop. Instance size and a maximum group size do not cause health check failures.","Domain 6: deployment troubleshooting",["Instance size does not cause health check failures when the app itself responds correctly on port 8080.",null,"A maximum size below desired capacity would cause a configuration error or cap the group, not repeatedly mark instances unhealthy.","Extra memory does not make an instance fail a health check; it only affects cost."],2],["cp84",0,6,"A nightly script that calls a cloud API thousands of times starts failing with HTTP 429 responses. What is the best fix?",["Retry immediately in a tight loop","Switch the API calls to plain HTTP","Add exponential backoff with jitter and reduce or batch requests","Grant the script's identity administrator rights so the API stops limiting its requests"],2,"HTTP 429 means Too Many Requests: the caller is being rate limited. Backoff and fewer calls respect the limit, while immediate retries make throttling worse and more permissions do not change rate limits.","Domain 6: performance troubleshooting",["Retrying immediately in a tight loop sends even more requests and makes the rate limiting worse.","Plain HTTP removes encryption and does not change the provider's rate limits, and many APIs reject it outright.",null,"Rate limits apply regardless of permissions; administrator rights would only break least privilege without stopping 429s."],2],["cp85",0,6,"A template deployment fails with an error that the regional vCPU limit for the instance family has been reached. What should the administrator do?",["Rewrite the template in another infrastructure-as-code language that the provider does not limit","Request a quota increase or use another region or instance family","Delete the virtual network and try again","Disable encryption on the volumes"],1,"Service quotas cap resources per region; raising the quota, freeing capacity or choosing a different region or family resolves the error. The IaC language and network are unrelated.","Domain 6: deployment troubleshooting",["Quotas apply to the resources in the account and region, not to the IaC language, so any tool would hit the same limit.",null,"Deleting the virtual network is unrelated to vCPU quotas and would break other resources.","Volume encryption has no effect on the regional vCPU quota for an instance family."],2],["cp86",0,6,"A user's IAM policy allows reading an object storage bucket, but requests still return 403 Access Denied. What should the administrator check first?",["Whether the bucket uses the archive tier","Whether the bucket policy or an organization policy contains an explicit deny","The user's desktop screen resolution","Whether the bucket has versioning enabled"],1,"In cloud policy evaluation an explicit deny anywhere overrides any allow. Versioning and storage tier do not produce a 403 for an allowed user.","Domain 6: access troubleshooting",["The archive tier affects retrieval time and cost, but an allowed user would not get a 403 just because of the tier.",null,"Desktop screen resolution has nothing to do with how cloud authorization policies are evaluated.","Versioning keeps prior object versions and does not deny access to users whose policy allows reads."],3],["cp87",0,6,"A website was moved to a new load balancer and the DNS record updated, but some users reach the old servers for hours. What is the most likely reason?",["The TLS certificate on the new load balancer uses a different key size than the old certificate did","The new load balancer is too small","The old record's TTL was long, so resolvers are still using cached answers","The web servers need more memory"],2,"Resolvers cache records for the TTL, so a long TTL delays the change. Lowering the TTL well before a migration shortens this window.","Domain 6: network troubleshooting",["A different key size on the certificate would not send users to the old servers; DNS decides which address they reach.","An undersized load balancer would cause slowness or errors, not route users to the old servers.",null,"Server memory does not affect which IP address users' resolvers return for the domain."],1],["cp88",0,6,"The cloud bill doubled this month. The cost report shows a large charge for storage volumes and public IP addresses that are not attached to any running VM. What is the cause?",["Too many IAM users","A security group misconfiguration that allowed too much traffic into the virtual network","Distributed tracing overhead","Orphaned resources left behind after VMs were deleted"],3,"Deleting a VM does not always delete its disks, snapshots or reserved IPs, and these keep billing. Security groups and IAM users are free.","Domain 6: cost troubleshooting",["IAM users do not incur charges, so their number cannot double the bill.","Security group rules are free; permissive rules are a security risk but do not create storage or IP charges.","Tracing may add some cost, but the report points to unattached volumes and IP addresses, not tracing data.",null],3],["cp89",0,6,"Overnight, browsers start showing security warnings for a company's API, and client scripts fail with TLS handshake errors. No code changed. What should be checked first?",["The container image size","The API's CPU utilization","The database backup schedule and retention settings","The TLS certificate's expiration date"],3,"A certificate expiring with no other change produces exactly these symptoms. Automating renewal and monitoring expiry dates prevents it.","Domain 6: access troubleshooting",["Container image size affects pull times and storage, not TLS handshakes or browser certificate warnings.","High CPU would cause slowness or timeouts, not certificate warnings in browsers.","Database backup settings have no role in the TLS handshake between clients and the API.",null],3],["cp90",0,6,"A pipeline that deployed successfully for months now fails at the deploy step with an authentication error, and no code has changed. What is the most likely cause?",["The Git repository has too many branches","The YAML file needs more comments","The service principal or access token used by the pipeline has expired or was rotated","The build agent has too much disk space"],2,"Credentials used by automation often expire or are rotated, which breaks pipelines with no code change. Branch count and comments do not affect authentication.","Domain 6: automation troubleshooting",["The number of branches in a repository does not affect whether the pipeline can authenticate to the deployment target.","YAML comments are ignored by the parser and cannot cause an authentication error.",null,"Plenty of free disk space on the build agent cannot cause an authentication failure; only a lack of space would cause errors."],2],["x1",0,1,"A private cloud team installs a hypervisor directly on bare-metal servers, with no general-purpose operating system underneath it. What type of hypervisor is this?",["Type 1 hypervisor","Type 2 hypervisor","Container runtime","Paravirtualized guest driver"],0,"A type 1 (bare-metal) hypervisor runs directly on the hardware and is what cloud and data center virtualization platforms use. A type 2 hypervisor is tempting but runs on top of a host OS, as desktop virtualization tools do.","Domain 1: Virtualization",[null,"A type 2 hypervisor runs as an application on top of a host operating system, which is exactly what this setup avoids.","A container runtime shares a host OS kernel to run containers; it does not virtualize hardware for full VMs.","Paravirtual drivers run inside guest VMs to improve I/O; they are not the layer installed on bare metal."],1],["x2",0,1,"A latency-sensitive trading app on shared cloud instances shows unpredictable spikes that correlate with heavy activity from other tenants on the same host. Which option most directly removes this multitenancy effect?",["Add more shared instances of the same size behind a load balancer","Switch the instances to spot pricing","Move the workload to dedicated hosts or dedicated instances","Enable encryption at rest on every attached volume"],2,"Dedicated hosts or instances run on hardware not shared with other customers, removing noisy neighbors. Adding more shared instances is tempting, but each one can still be affected by other tenants on its host.","Domain 1: Multitenancy",["More shared instances can still land beside busy tenants, so the noisy-neighbor effect remains.","Spot pricing changes the cost model and adds eviction risk; it does not isolate the hardware.",null,"Encryption protects stored data but has no effect on contention from other tenants."],2],["x3",0,1,"A company exposes 15 microservices to mobile apps. It wants one entry point that handles authentication, request throttling and routing to the correct service. What should it deploy?",["A network ACL on each subnet","An API gateway","A content delivery network","A NAT gateway in the public subnet"],1,"An API gateway gives clients a single front door and centralizes authentication, rate limiting, request routing and versioning for microservices. A CDN is tempting because it also sits in front of apps, but it focuses on caching content.","Domain 1: Microservices and API gateways",["Network ACLs filter packets by address and port; they cannot authenticate users or route by API path.",null,"A CDN caches static content near users; it is not designed to authenticate and throttle API calls to backends.","A NAT gateway lets private instances make outbound connections; it does not front inbound API traffic."],2],["x4",0,1,"An order service calls a slow invoicing service directly, and when invoicing is down, customer orders fail. Which design change lets orders succeed and invoices be processed later?",["Scale the order service to a larger instance","Merge both services into a single monolith","Add a CDN in front of the invoicing service","Put a message queue between the two services"],3,"A queue decouples producer and consumer: the order service adds a message and returns, and the invoicing service processes messages when it is available. Scaling the order service is tempting but does nothing about the dependency failing.","Domain 1: Event-driven architecture",["A larger order service still fails when its direct call to the invoicing service fails.","Combining them keeps the tight coupling, so an invoicing fault would still break orders.","A CDN caches content for readers; it cannot hold and retry work for a backend service.",null],2],["x5",0,1,"When an order is placed, the shipping, billing and analytics services must each receive their own copy of the event and process it independently. Which pattern fits best?",["A single queue that all three services read from","A nightly batch export to shared file storage","Publish/subscribe topic with a subscription per service","Synchronous REST calls from the order service"],2,"Pub/sub fans each event out to every subscriber, so all three services receive their own copy. A single shared queue is tempting, but its consumers compete, and each message is delivered to only one of them.","Domain 1: Event-driven architecture",["Consumers of one queue compete for messages, so each event would reach only one of the three services.","A nightly export delays processing and does not give each service its own event stream.",null,"Direct calls couple the order service to all three and fail if any of them is down."],3],["x6",0,1,"A video transcoding job reads very large files sequentially, and performance is limited by megabytes per second rather than by the number of small operations. Which storage metric should drive the volume choice?",["Throughput","IOPS","Object versioning","Recovery point objective"],0,"Large sequential workloads are bound by throughput (MB/s), so a throughput-optimized volume fits. IOPS is the tempting choice because it is the most quoted disk metric, but it matters most for small random I/O.","Domain 1: Storage performance",[null,"IOPS measures small random operations per second, which matters for databases more than for large sequential reads.","Versioning keeps prior copies of objects and has nothing to do with read speed.","RPO describes acceptable data loss after an incident, not disk performance."],3],["x7",0,1,"Two teams built VPCs that both use 10.0.0.0/16. They now need to peer the VPCs so their applications can communicate. What must happen first?",["Add a NAT gateway to each VPC","Enable DNS resolution across the peering","Create more subnets inside the existing ranges and retry the peering","Re-address one VPC so the CIDR ranges do not overlap"],3,"Peering requires non-overlapping CIDR blocks, because routers cannot tell which network an address belongs to. Adding NAT is tempting, but standard peering still refuses identical ranges, so planning distinct CIDRs up front is key.","Domain 1: CIDR planning",["NAT gateways provide outbound internet access; they do not make overlapping ranges peerable.","DNS settings do not fix routing conflicts from identical address ranges.","New subnets inside the same /16 still overlap with the other VPC.",null],2],["x8",0,1,"A company has 30 VPCs and two on-premises sites, and managing a full mesh of peering connections has become unworkable. Which design simplifies connectivity?",["More peering connections so that every single pair of VPCs is directly linked","A hub-and-spoke design using a transit gateway or hub network","A separate internet gateway in each of the VPCs","Merge all the workloads into one very large subnet"],1,"A transit hub connects every VPC and on-premises link once to a central point, which routes between them. Adding more peering is tempting, but mesh links grow with the square of the number of networks.","Domain 1: Hybrid connectivity and transit hubs",["A full mesh grows quickly with each new VPC and is exactly what has become unmanageable.",null,"Internet gateways give public internet access; they do not privately connect VPCs to each other.","One flat subnet removes segmentation and is not practical across teams and sites."],2],["x9",0,1,"A company runs its app in a primary region and a standby region. It wants DNS to send users to the standby automatically when health checks on the primary fail. Which DNS routing policy fits?",["Failover routing with health checks","Simple routing with one record","Weighted routing that splits traffic evenly","Geolocation routing based on the user's country"],0,"Failover routing returns the primary record while its health check passes and switches to the secondary when it fails. Weighted routing is tempting since it can use health checks, but it is designed to split traffic, not to hold a standby.","Domain 1: DNS routing",[null,"Simple routing always returns the same answer and cannot react to health checks.","Weighted routing divides traffic by percentage; it does not by itself make one region a standby.","Geolocation routes by where users are, not by whether the primary is healthy."],2],["x10",0,1,"A regulation requires that users in one country always be served by resources in that country's region, even if another region would respond faster. Which DNS routing policy enforces this?",["Latency-based routing","Weighted routing","Geolocation routing","Round-robin across all regions"],2,"Geolocation routing answers based on where the request comes from, so users in a country always get that country's endpoint. Latency-based routing is tempting for performance but selects the fastest region, which may be in another country.","Domain 1: DNS routing",["Latency routing picks the fastest region, which could send users outside their country and break the rule.","Weighted routing splits traffic by percentage, regardless of where the user is.",null,"Round-robin rotates answers with no regard to the user's location."],3],["x11",0,1,"The business says that after an outage it can accept losing at most 15 minutes of transactions. Which metric does this define?",["Recovery time objective (RTO)","Recovery point objective (RPO)","Mean time between failures (MTBF)","Service level objective for latency"],1,"RPO is the maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time, which drives how often data is backed up or replicated. RTO is the usual confusion, but it measures downtime, not lost data.","Domain 1: RPO and RTO",["RTO is how long the service may be down before it is restored, not how much data may be lost.",null,"MTBF measures the average time between failures, not tolerable data loss.","A latency SLO concerns response times, not data loss after an incident."],1],["x12",0,1,"A DR design keeps a complete but scaled-down copy of the application running in a second region, so it can take traffic within minutes after being scaled up. Which strategy is this?",["Pilot light","Backup and restore","Multisite active-active","Warm standby"],3,"Warm standby runs a smaller but fully functional copy that is scaled up during failover. Pilot light is tempting, but it keeps only the core, usually the data layer, running, with the application tier off.","Domain 1: Disaster recovery architectures",["Pilot light keeps only core pieces such as data replication running, with app servers off, so recovery takes longer.","Backup and restore rebuilds everything from backups after the disaster, which usually takes hours or more.","Active-active runs full capacity in both sites serving live traffic, with no scale-up step needed.",null],3],["x13",0,1,"Finance wants each department's cloud usage to be billed back to that department's budget, with the costs actually charged to it. What is this practice called?",["Showback","Right-sizing","Chargeback","Committed use"],2,"Chargeback allocates actual costs to the consuming departments' budgets, usually based on tags. Showback is the tempting look-alike, but it only shows departments what they used without charging them.","Domain 1: Cost models",["Showback only reports costs to departments without transferring the charges to their budgets.","Right-sizing changes resource sizes to match demand; it does not allocate costs.",null,"Committed use is a discount model for steady usage, not a way to bill departments."],1],["x14",0,1,"A company deliberately runs workloads on two different public cloud providers to avoid depending on a single vendor. Which deployment model is this?",["Multicloud","Hybrid cloud","Community cloud","Private cloud"],0,"Multicloud means using services from more than one public cloud provider, often to reduce vendor lock-in or use each provider's strengths. Hybrid is tempting, but it specifically mixes on-premises or private resources with public cloud.","Domain 1: Deployment models",[null,"Hybrid combines private or on-premises infrastructure with public cloud, not two public providers.","A community cloud is shared by organizations with common requirements, not spread across providers.","A private cloud is dedicated to one organization and does not describe using two public providers."],1],["x15",0,2,"Product managers want to show half of users a new checkout layout and half the old one, then keep whichever produces more completed purchases. Which release approach fits?",["Canary release","Blue-green deployment","In-place deployment","A/B testing"],3,"A/B testing runs two variants at the same time for different user groups and compares a business metric to pick the winner. Canary is tempting because it also splits traffic, but its goal is to limit risk from a new version.","Domain 2: Deployment strategies",["A canary sends a small share of traffic to a new version to catch errors before rollout, not to compare business outcomes.","Blue-green switches all traffic from one environment to another at once, so users are not compared side by side.","In-place replaces the running version on existing servers and gives no split between variants.",null],3],["x16",0,2,"A blue-green release has just switched all traffic to the green environment, and error rates jump. What is the fastest rollback?",["Rebuild the old version from source and redeploy it","Point traffic back to the blue environment","Restore last night's database backup","Scale the green environment to twice its size"],1,"Blue-green keeps the previous version running, so rollback is just redirecting traffic to it. Rebuilding the old version is tempting but slow and unnecessary when the blue environment still exists.","Domain 2: Rollback plans",["Rebuilding takes far longer than switching back to the blue environment, which is still running.",null,"Restoring a backup loses recent data and does not quickly restore the previous app version.","More capacity does not fix errors caused by the new release."],2],["x17",0,2,"During migration planning, a factory control application must stay on-premises because it needs very low latency to machines on the shop floor. Which migration strategy applies to it?",["Retain","Retire","Rehost","Repurchase"],0,"Retain means keeping an application where it is, for now, for reasons such as latency, compliance or recent investment. Retire is tempting because both leave the app out of the migration, but retire means shutting it down.","Domain 2: Migration strategies",[null,"Retire means decommissioning an application that is no longer needed, but this one is still required.","Rehosting would move it to the cloud, which conflicts with its latency needs.","Repurchase means replacing it with a SaaS product, which does not solve the need for local processing."],2],["x18",0,2,"A company rewrites a monolithic application into serverless functions and managed services to gain scalability. Which migration strategy is this?",["Rehost","Replatform","Refactor","Retain"],2,"Refactoring (re-architecting) changes the application's design and code to use cloud-native services. Replatform is tempting, but it involves only light changes rather than rewriting the architecture.","Domain 2: Migration strategies",["Rehost moves the application as it is, without code changes.","Replatforming makes small optimizations, such as moving to a managed database, without re-architecting.",null,"Retain keeps the application where it is, with no migration."],1],["x19",0,2,"A 2 TB production database must move to a managed cloud database with only a few minutes of downtime. Which approach fits best?",["Stop the app, take a full dump and restore it in the cloud","Initial copy plus continuous replication, then a short cutover","Ship the database on a transfer appliance and restore it","Point the app at the cloud database and let it fill up over time"],1,"Online migration copies the data first, then replicates ongoing changes until the target is in sync, so the cutover takes only minutes. Dump and restore is tempting for simplicity but means downtime for the entire copy.","Domain 2: Database migration",["A full dump and restore of 2 TB keeps the app offline for the whole transfer, far longer than a few minutes.",null,"An appliance takes days to ship, and changes made meanwhile would be lost without replication.","Starting with an empty database breaks the application because existing data is missing."],3],["x20",0,2,"A company is planning to migrate 300 applications in waves. Which applications are best for the first pilot wave?",["The most critical revenue systems, to get them done first","All 300 applications at once, to save time","The apps with the most dependencies, to test integrations","A few low-risk apps with few dependencies"],3,"A pilot wave uses simple, low-risk workloads to validate tools, runbooks and cutover steps before moving harder systems. Moving critical systems first is tempting because they matter most, but mistakes there are most costly.","Domain 2: Migration planning",["Starting with critical systems puts the business at risk while the team is still learning the process.","A single big-bang cutover removes the chance to learn and adjust between waves.","Heavily connected apps are complex and risky, a poor fit for a first pilot.",null],2],["x21",0,2,"A database volume must have all of its allocated space reserved on the storage array at creation, so it can never fail because the pool runs out of space. Which provisioning type is this?",["Thin provisioning","Object versioning","Thick provisioning","Deduplication"],2,"Thick provisioning reserves the full size of a volume up front, trading efficiency for guaranteed space. Thin provisioning is the opposite choice and risks write failures if the shared pool fills.","Domain 2: Provisioning storage",["Thin provisioning allocates space only as data is written, so an overcommitted pool can run out.","Versioning keeps prior copies of objects and does not reserve block storage space.",null,"Deduplication stores repeated data once to save space; it does not reserve capacity."],1],["x22",0,2,"Several engineers run the same Terraform configuration from their own laptops, and changes keep overwriting each other and corrupting state. What is the best fix?",["Store state in a shared remote backend with locking","Keep a separate local state file on each laptop","Email the latest state file to the whole team after each change is applied","Delete the state file before every run"],0,"A remote backend gives everyone one shared state, and locking stops two runs from changing it at once. Separate local state files are tempting, but they guarantee drift between engineers and the real infrastructure.","Domain 2: Infrastructure as code state",[null,"Separate local states drift apart, and each engineer's runs would conflict with real resources.","Manual sharing is error-prone and does not stop two runs from happening at the same time.","Without state, the tool loses track of existing resources and may try to recreate them."],3],["x23",0,2,"The same IaC template must deploy small instances in development and larger ones in production. What is the cleanest way to handle the difference?",["Keep a separate copy of the template per environment","Resize the production instances by hand after deploying","Hard-code the largest size for every environment","Use parameters with a values file per environment"],3,"Parameters let one template accept different values, such as instance size, per environment. Keeping separate copies is tempting but leads to templates that slowly diverge.","Domain 2: Templates and parameters",["Copies drift apart over time, which defeats the consistency IaC provides.","Manual changes after deployment cause drift and are lost at the next deployment.","Using production sizes everywhere wastes money in development.",null],2],["x24",0,2,"A developer's test script accidentally deleted production resources because both environments are in the same cloud account. Which change best prevents this?",["Add a naming convention to all production resources","Separate production into its own account with its own access","Run all test scripts only at night","Give developers the account owner role so they can fix mistakes"],1,"Separate accounts or subscriptions for each environment create a strong boundary for access and blast radius. A naming convention is tempting but gives no enforcement; permissions are what matter.","Domain 2: Environment separation",["Names alone do not stop a script with permissions from deleting resources.",null,"Changing the timing does not reduce the chance of deleting production resources.","More privileges widen the blast radius rather than reducing it."],2],["x25",0,2,"After a new application goes live, the team records its normal CPU use, response times and error rates. Why?",["To have a baseline to compare future performance against","To qualify for the provider's SLA credits if the service goes down later","To replace the need for alerting","To meet a requirement of the migration tool"],0,"A performance baseline shows what normal looks like, making it possible to spot regressions and set meaningful alert thresholds. Replacing alerting is the tempting error, because baselines actually feed into alerting.","Domain 2: Post-deployment validation",[null,"SLA credits depend on the provider's availability, not on the customer's baseline.","Baselines help set alert thresholds; they do not replace alerting.","Migration tools do not require post-deployment baselines to function."],1],["x26",0,2,"A product page runs the same expensive database query thousands of times a minute, and results change only every few minutes. Which managed service best reduces database load?",["A larger database instance","An archive storage tier for the table","A managed in-memory cache in front of the database","A second availability zone for the database"],2,"Caching the query result in a managed in-memory store serves repeated reads quickly and offloads the database. A larger instance is tempting but pays to repeat identical work instead of avoiding it.","Domain 2: Deploying managed services",["A bigger instance absorbs more load but keeps running the same repeated query, which is costly and does not remove the waste.","Archive storage is for rarely read data and would make reads slower, not faster.",null,"A standby in another zone improves availability but does not serve read traffic."],2],["x27",0,3,"Backups run as a full backup on Sunday and incremental backups Monday through Wednesday nights. The server fails Thursday morning. What is needed to restore it?",["Only Wednesday's incremental backup","Sunday's full and all three incrementals","Sunday's full and only Wednesday's incremental","Only Sunday's full backup"],1,"Incremental backups each capture changes since the last backup of any type, so restore needs the full plus every incremental in order. Using only the latest incremental with the full is the tempting mix-up with differential backups.","Domain 3: Backup types",["An incremental holds only changes since the previous backup, so it cannot restore the server by itself.",null,"That works for differentials; incrementals each hold only one day's changes, so Monday and Tuesday are also needed.","The full backup alone would lose all changes made Monday through Wednesday."],2],["x28",0,3,"What does the 3-2-1 backup rule recommend?",["Three backups a day, kept two weeks, one restore test","Three regions, two providers, one encryption key","Three full backups, two incrementals, one snapshot","Three copies, two media types, one copy off-site"],3,"The 3-2-1 rule keeps three copies of data on two different types of media, with one copy off-site. Modern versions often add an immutable or offline copy; it is not about how often backups run.","Domain 3: The 3-2-1 rule",["The rule counts copies and locations, not backup frequency or retention.","The rule does not require multiple regions or providers, and it says nothing about keys.","The rule is about copies and media, not a mix of backup types.",null],1],["x29",0,3,"Snapshots are taken of a busy database VM's disk without pausing writes or informing the database. After a restore test, the database needs recovery and some recent transactions are missing. What is the best improvement?",["Take the same crash-consistent snapshots much more often, every few minutes","Move the snapshots to a cheaper storage tier","Use application-consistent snapshots that quiesce the database","Replace the snapshots with a stronger encryption key"],2,"Application-consistent snapshots flush and pause writes (quiesce) so the database is captured in a clean state. More frequent crash-consistent snapshots are tempting, but each still behaves like pulling the power cord.","Domain 3: Snapshots and backups",["More frequent crash-consistent snapshots still capture the database mid-write.","The storage tier affects cost, not whether the captured data is consistent.",null,"Encryption protects confidentiality but does not make the database state consistent."],3],["x30",0,3,"An autoscaling policy is set to keep average CPU across the group at 50%, adding or removing instances as needed. Which policy type is this?",["Target tracking","Scheduled scaling","Manual scaling","Vertical scaling"],0,"Target tracking adjusts the number of instances to keep a metric near a chosen value, such as 50% CPU. Scheduled scaling is tempting as another automatic policy, but it acts by time rather than by a metric.","Domain 3: Autoscaling policies",[null,"Scheduled scaling changes capacity at set times, not by holding a metric at a target.","Manual scaling requires an administrator to change capacity.","Vertical scaling resizes instances instead of changing their number."],1],["x31",0,3,"An autoscaling group adds instances, then removes them a minute later, then adds them again, in a constant cycle. New instances need three minutes to warm up. What should be adjusted first?",["Lower the scale-out threshold","Switch to a larger instance type","Remove the maximum instance limit","Lengthen the cooldown or warm-up period"],3,"A cooldown or warm-up period stops the policy from acting again before new instances affect the metric, which prevents flapping. Lowering the threshold is tempting but makes the group react even faster.","Domain 3: Autoscaling and cooldowns",["A lower threshold triggers scaling even sooner and can make the flapping worse.","Larger instances may help later but do not address the timing mismatch causing the cycle.","Removing the upper limit adds cost risk and does not stop repeated scaling.",null],3],["x32",0,3,"A provider's SLA promises 99.9% monthly availability. The operations team sets an internal goal of 99.95% for its service. What is this internal goal called?",["A service level agreement (SLA)","A service level objective (SLO)","A recovery time objective (RTO)","A key management policy"],1,"An SLO is a measurable internal reliability target, often stricter than any external SLA to leave a safety margin. SLA is the tempting pick, but it is the contract, not the internal goal.","Domain 3: SLAs and SLOs",["An SLA is a contractual commitment to customers with penalties, not an internal target.",null,"RTO is how long a restore may take after a disaster, not an ongoing availability target.","Key management policies govern encryption keys and have nothing to do with availability goals."],2],["x33",0,3,"Roughly how much downtime does a 99.99% availability target allow in a 30-day month?",["About 4 minutes","About 43 minutes","About 7 hours","About 26 seconds"],0,"A 30-day month has 43,200 minutes, and 0.01% of that is about 4.3 minutes. 43 minutes is the tempting answer because it is the familiar figure for 99.9%.","Domain 3: Availability math",[null,"That is the allowance for 99.9%; adding another nine cuts it by a factor of ten.","That is the allowance for 99%, far more than 99.99% permits.","That is close to the allowance for 99.999%, one more nine than asked."],2],["x34",0,3,"A request must pass through a web tier and an API tier, each with 99.9% availability, and fails if either tier is down. What is the approximate availability of the combined path?",["99.9%, the same as each tier","99.99%, because there are two tiers","About 99.8%","About 99.95%, the average"],2,"For components in series, multiply their availabilities: 0.999 x 0.999 is about 0.998, or 99.8%. Expecting 99.99% is tempting, but that math applies to redundant parallel components, not a chain.","Domain 3: Availability math",["When both tiers are needed, their downtime adds up, so the combined figure is lower than either tier.","Adding tiers in series lowers availability; only redundant copies of one tier raise it.",null,"Availability of components in series multiplies; it is not averaged."],3],["x35",0,3,"A web service occasionally hangs, and each time an engineer restarts it by hand at night. What is the best operational improvement?",["Page more engineers so the restart happens faster","An automated runbook that restarts it when a health check fails","Disable the health check to stop the alerts","Double the instance size"],1,"Self-healing automation reacts to a failed health check by running the known fix, such as a restart, and logs it for later root-cause work. Paging more people is tempting but keeps the fix manual.","Domain 3: Operational automation",["More people on call still means manual work and delay rather than automatic recovery.",null,"Turning off the check hides the problem and removes the signal needed for recovery.","More resources may not fix the hang, and it still needs a manual restart when it happens."],2],["x36",0,3,"A critical vulnerability is announced in the base image used by the company's production containers. What is the recommended way to patch them?",["Run the package manager inside each running container","Restart the containers so they pick up host patches","Wait for the next major application release","Rebuild from the patched base image and redeploy"],3,"Containers are treated as immutable: rebuild the image on the patched base, scan it, and redeploy replacements. Patching inside running containers is tempting but the fix disappears when the container is recreated.","Domain 3: Patch management for containers",["Changes inside running containers are lost on restart and make containers differ from their image.","Restarting reuses the same vulnerable image, since the flaw is in the image, not the host.","Waiting leaves a known critical vulnerability exposed in production.",null],2],["x37",0,4,"Attack traffic is coming from one IP range. The team's security groups support only allow rules and are applied per instance. How can the team block the range for an entire subnet?",["Add a deny rule to each security group","Remove the subnet's route to the internet gateway","Add a deny rule for the range to the subnet's network ACL","Enable encryption in transit on the load balancer"],2,"Network ACLs apply at the subnet level and support explicit deny rules, making them the right place to block a specific range. Security groups are tempting because they are the usual firewall, but here they allow only permit rules.","Domain 4: Security groups vs network ACLs",["The stem states the security groups support only allow rules, so a deny cannot be added there.","Removing the route cuts off all internet traffic, not just the attacking range.",null,"Encryption protects data in transit but does not block traffic from any source."],3],["x38",0,4,"A regulation requires that encryption keys be generated and stored in dedicated, single-tenant hardware that only the company controls. Which option meets this?",["A dedicated cloud HSM service","The provider's default encryption with provider-managed keys","A secrets manager holding the key as a string","Encrypting keys with a password in a config file"],0,"A dedicated cloud HSM gives the customer single-tenant hardware security modules under its exclusive control. Provider-managed default encryption is tempting because it is simple, but the provider controls those keys.","Domain 4: Key management and HSMs",[null,"Provider-managed keys are controlled by the provider, not exclusively by the company.","Storing a key as a secret does not give dedicated key hardware.","Keys in files are easy to leak and are not held in hardware."],2],["x39",0,4,"An application encrypts each file with its own data key, then encrypts that data key with a master key held in a KMS. What is this technique called, and why is it used?",["Tokenization, so the data can be mapped back later by a secure vault","Hashing, so the files cannot be decrypted by anyone","TLS, so the files are protected only while in transit","Envelope encryption, so large data never goes to the KMS"],3,"Envelope encryption encrypts data locally with a data key and protects that key with a KMS master key, so only small keys go to the KMS. Tokenization is tempting as another protection method, but it replaces values rather than encrypting them.","Domain 4: Encryption and key management",["Tokenization swaps values for surrogates held in a vault; it does not involve wrapping data keys.","Hashing is one-way; these files must be decryptable, and keys are involved.","TLS protects network connections, not files stored with wrapped keys.",null],3],["x40",0,4,"A web app connects to a managed database over the network, and an auditor requires that the traffic cannot be read if intercepted. What should be enforced?",["Encryption at rest on the database volumes","TLS on all connections to the database","Daily snapshots of the database","A larger database instance class"],1,"Encryption in transit, typically TLS, protects data moving between the app and the database from interception. Encryption at rest is tempting, but it only protects data once it is stored.","Domain 4: Encryption in transit",["At-rest encryption protects stored data, not packets moving over the network.",null,"Snapshots help recovery but do nothing to protect data in transit.","The instance size has no effect on whether traffic is encrypted."],1],["x41",0,4,"A company wants to apply DLP and encryption rules to its cloud file shares but does not know which files hold sensitive data. What should it do first?",["Discover and classify the data by sensitivity","Encrypt every file with a separate key","Block all external sharing permanently","Move all files to the archive tier"],0,"Data classification identifies which data is sensitive so that DLP, encryption and access rules can be targeted at it. Encrypting everything is tempting but skips the step that tells you what really needs protection.","Domain 4: Data classification and DLP",[null,"Encrypting without knowing sensitivity adds complexity and still leaves DLP rules untargeted.","A blanket block disrupts business and still does not show where sensitive data is.","Archiving changes cost and access speed, not protection of sensitive data."],2],["x42",0,4,"A public website is flooded with huge volumes of traffic from many sources, exhausting its bandwidth. Which control is designed for this?",["A secrets manager","Customer-managed encryption keys","The provider's DDoS protection service","A CIS hardening benchmark"],2,"DDoS protection services detect and absorb volumetric attacks at the provider's network edge. Hardening is tempting as a general security step, but it does not deal with traffic volume.","Domain 4: DDoS protection",["A secrets manager stores credentials and has no role in absorbing traffic floods.","Encryption keys protect data confidentiality, not availability during a flood.",null,"Hardening reduces host attack surface but cannot absorb network floods."],1],["x43",0,4,"Several engineers hold permanent administrator rights but only need them for a few hours each month. What best reduces this standing access?",["Share one admin account among all the engineers","Just-in-time elevation that is approved and time-limited","Require longer passwords for the admin accounts","Remove MFA to make elevation faster"],1,"Just-in-time access grants admin rights only when needed, for a limited time and often with approval, which enforces least privilege. Longer passwords are tempting but do not change how long privileges exist.","Domain 4: Least privilege",["Shared accounts remove accountability and still leave permanent admin access.",null,"Longer passwords help but leave the permanent privileges in place.","Removing MFA weakens security and does nothing about standing privileges."],2],["x44",0,4,"A team wants to catch misconfigurations such as public buckets or open management ports before cloud resources are ever created. Where should scanning happen?",["Scan running VMs once a quarter","Review the monthly cloud bill for unusual items","Wait for the provider to email a warning","Scan IaC templates in the pipeline before deploying"],3,"Scanning infrastructure-as-code templates in CI finds misconfigurations before they are deployed, known as shifting left. Scanning running hosts is tempting but only finds problems after deployment.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management for IaC",["Quarterly host scans find issues long after deployment and miss template-level settings.","Billing does not reveal misconfigurations such as open ports.","Relying on provider emails is reactive and happens only after resources exist.",null],2],["x45",0,4,"A CI/CD pipeline deploys to the cloud using a long-lived access key stored as a pipeline secret. The security team wants to eliminate stored cloud keys entirely. What should replace it?",["Rotate the access key every week","Store the key in a different pipeline variable","Workload identity federation using short-lived tokens","Use the root account credentials instead"],2,"Federation lets the pipeline exchange its own identity token (for example via OpenID Connect) for short-lived cloud credentials, so no key is stored. Weekly rotation is tempting, but a stored key still exists between rotations.","Domain 4: Workload identities",["Rotation shortens exposure but still leaves a stored long-lived key, which the team wants to remove.","Moving the key keeps a stored credential and does not reduce risk.",null,"Root credentials are the most powerful and should never be used for automation."],3],["x46",0,4,"Which action is part of hardening a new server image?",["Disabling unused services and default accounts","Opening all ports for easier troubleshooting","Installing extra tools in case they are needed","Turning off logging to improve performance"],0,"Hardening reduces attack surface by removing or disabling unneeded services, ports, software and default accounts, often following a CIS benchmark. Installing extra tools is tempting for convenience but adds risk.","Domain 4: Hardening",[null,"Opening ports expands the attack surface, the opposite of hardening.","Extra software adds attack surface; hardening removes what is not needed.","Logging is needed for detection and audits, so disabling it weakens security."],1],["x47",0,4,"Auditors worry that an attacker who gains admin access to a production account could delete the audit logs to hide activity. What best protects the logs?",["Keep the logs in the same production account but in a larger bucket","Compress the logs to save space","Store logs only on the instances that produce them","Send logs to a separate account with write-once retention"],3,"Centralizing logs in a separate, restricted account with immutable (write-once) retention stops a compromised workload account from erasing evidence. Keeping them in the same account is tempting, but its admins could delete them.","Domain 4: Audit logs and governance",["Logs in the same account can still be deleted by a compromised admin there.","Compression lowers storage cost and does not stop deletion.","Local logs disappear when instances are deleted or compromised.",null],2],["x48",0,5,"A team wants to permanently mark the exact commit that shipped as release 2.3.0 so it can be checked out later. What should it create in Git?",["A fork","A tag","A stash","A merge conflict"],1,"Git tags mark a specific commit with a name such as v2.3.0, which is the standard way to identify releases. A branch could also point to it, but branches move as commits are added, while a release tag stays fixed.","Domain 5: Source control",["A fork copies the whole repository, not a marker on one commit.",null,"A stash saves uncommitted local changes temporarily; it does not mark released commits.","A merge conflict is a situation to resolve, not a way to label a release."],1],["x49",0,5,"Every change that passes the automated tests is released to production automatically, with no manual approval step. What is this called?",["Continuous deployment","Continuous delivery","Continuous integration","Blue-green deployment"],0,"Continuous deployment automatically releases every passing change to production. Continuous delivery is the tempting look-alike, but it keeps a manual approval before production.","Domain 5: Continuous delivery vs deployment",[null,"Continuous delivery keeps code releasable but requires a manual decision to push to production.","Continuous integration covers building and testing merged code, not releasing it to production.","Blue-green is a way to switch traffic between environments, not the name of a fully automated pipeline."],1],["x50",0,5,"An Ansible playbook is run twice in a row against the same servers. On the second run it reports no changes. Which property does this show?",["Immutability","Elasticity","Idempotency","Concurrency"],2,"An idempotent operation produces the same result however many times it runs, so a second run finds nothing to change. Immutability is tempting because both relate to consistent servers, but it means replacing rather than reconfiguring.","Domain 5: Configuration management",["Immutability means replacing servers rather than changing them, not re-running safely with no changes.","Elasticity is automatic scaling of resources with demand.",null,"Concurrency is running multiple tasks at once, unrelated to repeated runs having the same result."],2],["x51",0,5,"Before applying an infrastructure-as-code change to production, an engineer wants to see exactly which resources will be created, changed or destroyed. What should they run?",["An immediate apply followed by a review of the results","A plan or preview of the change","A delete of the state file to start fresh","A manual inventory of the resources in the console"],1,"IaC tools offer a plan or preview (such as terraform plan or a what-if operation) that lists proposed changes without applying them. Applying and then reviewing is tempting for speed but gives no chance to stop a destructive change.","Domain 5: Infrastructure as code tools",["Applying first makes the changes before anyone reviews them, which defeats the purpose.",null,"Deleting state causes the tool to lose track of resources and may lead to duplicates.","A manual inventory shows what exists now, not what the change will do."],2],["x52",0,5,"A team wants to add explanatory comments inside its configuration files. Which format supports comments natively?",["JSON","CSV","Base64","YAML"],3,"YAML supports comments that start with #, which is one reason it is popular for pipelines and playbooks. JSON is the tempting choice because it is widely used for config, but the standard does not allow comments.","Domain 5: Data formats",["Standard JSON has no comment syntax, so comments make it invalid.","CSV is a tabular data format with no standard comment syntax.","Base64 is an encoding for binary data, not a configuration format.",null],2],["x53",0,5,"Which configuration management tool traditionally uses an agent installed on each managed node that pulls its configuration from a central server?",["Ansible","Git","Puppet","cURL"],2,"Puppet traditionally uses agents on nodes that periodically pull configuration from a Puppet server. Ansible is tempting as the best-known tool, but it is agentless and push-based.","Domain 5: Configuration management",["Ansible is typically agentless and pushes changes over SSH or WinRM.","Git is a version control system, not a configuration management tool.",null,"cURL is a command-line tool for HTTP requests and does not manage server configuration."],2],["x54",0,6,"VPC A is peered with VPC B, and VPC B is peered with VPC C. Instances in A cannot reach instances in C, although all security groups allow the traffic. What is the most likely cause?",["Peering is not transitive, so A and C need their own link","The security groups on the instances in VPC B are blocking the traffic","DNS resolution is disabled on VPC C","The NAT gateway in VPC A is out of capacity"],0,"Standard VPC peering does not route traffic through a middle VPC, so A and C need a direct peering or a transit hub. Blaming VPC B's security groups is tempting, but traffic never passes through B's instances.","Domain 6: Network troubleshooting",[null,"Traffic from A to C does not flow through B's instances, and the stem says security groups allow it.","DNS problems would affect names, but even direct IP connections fail here.","NAT gateways handle outbound internet traffic, not private traffic between peered VPCs."],3],["x55",0,6,"A private endpoint was created for a storage service, but VMs still reach the service over the internet. A lookup of the service's name from a VM returns a public IP address. What is the most likely cause?",["The storage account is in the wrong access tier","The VMs need a larger instance size","The security group blocks outbound HTTPS","The private DNS zone is not linked to the VMs' network"],3,"Private endpoints rely on DNS returning the private IP, usually through a private DNS zone linked to the virtual network. A security group issue is tempting, but the symptom is resolution to the public address, which points to DNS.","Domain 6: DNS and private endpoints",["The access tier affects cost and retrieval, not which IP address DNS returns.","Instance size has no effect on name resolution.","If HTTPS were blocked, the VMs could not reach the service at all.",null],3],["x56",0,6,"After a team enabled replication of a large storage bucket to another region, the cloud bill shows a sharp rise in data transfer charges. What explains this?",["Inbound traffic to the primary bucket became billable","Inter-region replication traffic is billed","Storing data in two regions is billed as transfer","Replication doubles the price of API requests"],1,"Providers usually charge for data transferred between regions, so replicating large amounts of data raises transfer costs. Blaming inbound traffic is tempting, but ingress is generally free while inter-region movement is not.","Domain 6: Cost and billing anomalies",["Data transfer into the cloud is typically free; the new charge comes from copying between regions.",null,"The second copy adds storage charges, but data transfer charges come from moving the data.","Request pricing does not double because of replication; data movement is the driver."],2],["x57",0,6,"A bug caused 100% CPU on every instance, and autoscaling kept launching new instances overnight until the bill spiked. Which safeguard would have limited the cost?",["A maximum group size plus billing alerts","A shorter DNS TTL on the load balancer","Customer-managed encryption keys","Longer log retention for the group"],0,"A sensible maximum size caps how far autoscaling can grow, and budget or billing alerts warn when spending jumps. Longer log retention is tempting because it helps analysis, but it would not have stopped the runaway cost.","Domain 6: Runaway autoscaling",[null,"DNS TTLs affect how quickly clients see record changes, not how many instances are launched.","Encryption keys protect data and have no effect on scaling or spending.","Keeping logs longer helps investigations but does not cap spending."],2],["x58",0,6,"A VM's application is slow even though its own CPU use is moderate. Monitoring inside the guest shows high CPU steal time. What does this indicate?",["A memory leak in the application","A misconfigured DNS server","Contention on the physical host from other workloads","An expired TLS certificate"],2,"Steal time measures how long the VM waited for the hypervisor to give it physical CPU, which points to host contention. Fixes include changing instance type or using dedicated capacity. A memory leak is tempting but shows different symptoms.","Domain 6: Performance troubleshooting",["A memory leak shows as rising memory use, not CPU steal time.","DNS problems cause name resolution delays, not steal time.",null,"Certificate expiry causes handshake errors, not slow CPU scheduling."],3],["x59",0,6,"A deployment pipeline that worked yesterday now fails with errors from the IaC tool's provider plugin. Nobody changed the templates, but the pipeline always downloads the newest plugin. What is the best fix?",["Delete and recreate all the infrastructure","Pin the plugin to a known-good version","Give the pipeline identity administrator rights","Disable all tests in the pipeline"],1,"Pinning dependency versions keeps builds repeatable, and upgrades are made deliberately and tested. Granting admin rights is tempting when a pipeline fails, but the symptom points to an unplanned version change.","Domain 6: Automation and dependency failures",["Recreating resources is risky and does not stop the next unplanned plugin upgrade.",null,"Permissions are not the cause; the change in plugin version is.","Turning off tests hides failures and does not fix the version mismatch."],2],["x60",0,6,"Several unrelated services in one region start failing at the same moment, and no changes were deployed. What should an administrator check early on?",["The Git history of the application","The expiry dates of user passwords","The storage tier of the log bucket","The provider's service health dashboard"],3,"Simultaneous failures across unrelated services in one region suggest a provider-side incident, which the provider's health dashboard would show. Reviewing Git history is tempting, but nothing was deployed.","Domain 6: Using provider health dashboards",["With no deployments and several unrelated services failing, a code change is unlikely.","Password expiry would not break many unrelated services at once.","The log bucket's tier does not cause widespread service failures.",null],1]]);
